00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.600
And now, for my discussion
with Ido Portal.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.780
Ido, thank you for coming here today.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.490
I've been looking forward
to sitting down with you

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.800
to talk for a very long time.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.530
I was first exposed to your work

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:02.220
from my post or a podcast I believe

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.300
of you had a group of people
walking down handrails,

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.220
literally the handrails along stairwells.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.890
And as a, I don't want to
say former skateboarder,

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.080
once a skateboarder,
always a skateboarder.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.620
As a skateboarder, handrails
have a particular meaning,

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:20.013
but I was really struck by, first of all,

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:24.270
the incredible range of
skill that people had

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.480
and yet their willingness to do this.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.790
Right, I think of handrails
and walking on handrails

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:32.250
or skateboarding on handrails
as a potential hazard,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.430
and yet some of the incredible proficiency

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:37.530
that some of the people
there including yourself had.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:40.077
So, like many people, I
was drawn to your practice

00:08:40.077 --> 00:08:43.320
and your work initially
through a wide-eyed, "Wow."

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:44.970
You know, they're doing
some incredible stuff

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:46.320
on natural objects,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.140
much as skateboarders or parkour folks do.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.200
But over the years, we've
been in communication

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.650
and I've come to realize that
you're a true intellectual

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.360
of the topic of movement.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.650
And I define it intellectual

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:00.690
as somebody who can understand a topic

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:02.940
at multiple levels of granularity,

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.570
detail, general, specific,
connections, et cetera.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.973
So, to start off,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.290
could you share with us your conception

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.720
of this idea of movement?

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.880
You know, obviously movement

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.850
involves translation through space,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.910
but when you talk about
a movement practice,

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.740
what are you really thinking about?

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:26.820
What are we talking about

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.103
when we talk about a movement practice?

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:33.213
- It's a big question.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:38.880
I somehow left the definition,

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:43.880
the very tight definition
of it out for myself

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.410
because I felt it was
starting to constrict me

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:47.243
and be around me

00:09:47.243 --> 00:09:49.953
and I let the practice
itself really define it.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:55.950
But I think part of
our sense of everything

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:58.350
is actually a sense of movement

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:02.370
and then the stillness in
the background of that.

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:06.570
So, for me, this is the entity
that I refer to as movement,

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:11.570
and using that perspective for
self-evolution development,

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:14.610
of course, the physical side,

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.350
but also movement of emotions,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:21.350
movement of thoughts, and
any other movement streams.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.210
And by switching these layers

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:26.250
and examining it from different places,

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.383
you get a better and better sense of it.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.580
I think the visuals nowadays and media

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:36.240
are what defines for people
in the beginning things.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.100
And then little by little with experience,

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.260
they can dive deeper, which is good.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:45.090
There is some aspect, sexy
aspect or not so sexy aspect,

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:48.150
and then you pull on it
and you start to examine

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:53.150
and dive deeper, and then
you receive the gift of it,

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:54.963
finding out more.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:56.970
- I heard you say once

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.190
that we are not just a brain with a body,

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:02.070
but we are a body with a brain,

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:05.280
which I absolutely love
because as a student

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.410
and a researcher of the nervous system,

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:10.770
I never think about the brain
as its own isolated thing,

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:11.603
I think about the nervous system

00:11:11.603 --> 00:11:13.830
and the fact that the
brain and the spinal cord

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:14.663
are connected to the body,

00:11:14.663 --> 00:11:17.787
and the body is connected
to this, to the brain.

00:11:17.787 --> 00:11:19.290
And in every direction,

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.990
it's everything truly is connected

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:25.020
at the physical level,
physiological level.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.820
Could you just share for a moment

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.460
how you think about this
body-brain relationship

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:34.350
in terms of you mentioned
movement of emotions,

00:11:34.350 --> 00:11:35.580
movement of the body,

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.920
that you can't really separate the two.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.980
And for the typical person
who's listening to this,

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:44.070
they might not immediately
understand what that means.

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:46.020
Maybe it's something that
has to be experienced,

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.620
but when we think about
the body and the brain

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:52.440
and the whole thing working
as one cohesive whole,

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.059
what does that mean to you?

00:11:54.059 --> 00:11:55.860
Or put simply,

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.820
when you do a movement practice
what are you focusing on?

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:02.040
Are you focusing on the
movement of your limbs?

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.180
I have to imagine that's true,

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.580
but are you also focusing
on how that makes you feel

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.983
or how your feelings make you move?

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:13.143
- Okay, okay, so some thoughts,

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.920
I will try not to answer
any of your questions

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.000
during this interview,

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:20.430
but I will definitely give some thoughts

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:21.963
and then we can play with it.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:27.840
I think these definitions

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.870
and, in general, the limitation of words

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:33.900
ends up creating

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.987
some kind of a corruptive process.

00:12:36.987 --> 00:12:41.910
You know, words corrupt us
and corrupt our understanding.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:43.440
So, I think the brain-body,

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:46.630
this Cartesian state of mind and thinking

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:52.110
brought a lot of good, but
also brought a lot of problems.

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:53.490
And movement, for me,

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:56.520
is the entity that ties
everything together.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:00.231
It's the magic, it's the thoughts anima,

00:13:00.231 --> 00:13:03.000
it's when the coin spins

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.270
and you see both sides
appear at the same time.

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:07.650
It's a beautiful analogy

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:10.593
from a friend of mine, Dr. Rasmus Olme.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:16.530
So, the mind and body
are one of those pairs,

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:19.500
and I call it the
movement/body/mind system,

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:23.700
so it's when it's
integrated, it's in motion.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.340
There is also a stillness
that appears there, of course,

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.860
and without it there can be no motion,

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:33.860
but maybe that is a very good way

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:35.340
to start to think of things.

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:38.250
There is no really pure mental processes,

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:39.360
cognitive processes.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.463
There is no pure physical processes,

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:44.310
everything touches everything,

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:47.403
there is a wholeness, and
that wholeness is in motion.

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:53.910
Yeah, the movement
practice takes these beats

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:54.900
and examined them.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:58.230
And here is a pragmatic thing,

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:03.230
the scientist, the cerebral
thinking about movement,

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:05.760
this is important.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:09.930
The emotional side coloring,

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.510
feeling the colors and
the textures of motion.

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:15.030
A lot of people who are involved
with a movement practice

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:17.430
never end up feeling motions,

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:19.890
really focusing on how it makes you feel

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:21.990
or how it feels itself.

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.540
And then the actual movement, the action,

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.510
so, it's action, emotion, and thought.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:29.730
And those are three streams of movement

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.080
and they interlay together

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:35.910
into this kind of a braided experience

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.017
and whole experience.

00:14:37.017 --> 00:14:40.322
And I try to bring all these
aspects into my practice

00:14:40.322 --> 00:14:41.973
and the way that I live my life.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:48.230
- I think most people who
embark on a movement practice

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:52.560
will first want to know
which movements to do, right?

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:56.910
Squats, planks, push-ups,

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:58.560
pirouettes, right?

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:01.320
Pick your movement, it
could be any movement.

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:05.880
Are there any sort of
just basic entry points

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:09.210
that you believe everybody
should walk through

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:11.640
as they embrace a movement
practice the first time

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.400
and maybe even every time
they do a movement practice?

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.670
I mean, earlier today I
had the great privilege

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:21.540
of being guided through a long
series of movement practices,

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.910
and yet, the first
practice we did involve,

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:27.330
at first anyway, stillness not movement.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:31.650
So, if you would, could
you inform us how people

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:34.980
should think about approaching
a movement practice?

00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:39.980
What is the first layer of
any good movement practice?

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.060
- So, you touched the word movement,

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:44.460
and it's important for me to separate it

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:47.523
from the word Movement with a capital M.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.880
Movements are the containers

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:53.490
and movement is the content,

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:56.040
and the content cannot be carried

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:58.530
in any way without containers.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:01.800
So, the first entry point
is to choose containers,

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.870
and then the second thing to make sure

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:08.490
is to put specific content
into those containers

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:10.530
and then enjoy them.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:12.990
I tell people that it's
like a cup of water

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:14.670
and you're being handed that cup of water.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:19.500
And nowadays very often people
will start to chew on the cup

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:20.940
instead of drinking the water,

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:23.460
making it yours and discard the cup.

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:26.580
And then, maybe later you want
to have bone broth or soup

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:29.430
so you use a different container, a bowl.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:32.520
So, a movement practice to
start can start from anywhere,

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:36.360
it's a rhizome, it's an open system,

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.730
it has no center, it's decentralized,

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:40.800
and it can be approached from anywhere,

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:45.390
and that's its magic and
that's the benefit of it.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:49.530
Some people find the
body a good entry point,

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.873
some people don't even
enter from the body.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:55.800
Sometimes you can enter
from other perspectives

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.060
and then inside the body,

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.340
for example, where should we enter?

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:01.800
If we decided to take the body approach,

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:03.600
the spine can be a nice decision,

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.733
but some will choose just the pelvis.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:09.510
Any one of those points are valid.

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:11.970
And then, playfulness
can be an entry point,

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:15.060
an attribute, and this is so open.

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:19.650
So, I don't want to limit
people and limit their minds

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:21.570
in the way that they
engage with the practice,

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:26.570
but I also want to
encourage the self-inquiry.

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:29.370
Am I doing movements practice,

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:32.220
or am I doing a movement practice?

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.050
- So, could you help
me distinguish the two

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:34.920
a little bit further?

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:36.120
I think I understand the difference

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:39.060
between a sort of the
noun versus the verbs.

00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:42.720
And in some ways here we are
dealing with the challenge

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:45.780
of the barriers that language present

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:47.040
to something that's physical, right?

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:51.510
I mean, indeed, there may not be a...

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:52.343
I have to assume

00:17:52.343 --> 00:17:55.560
there is no perfect verbal
language for movement.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:57.630
There are certain movements
that defy language.

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:01.140
I could say somebody jumped
at a particular trajectory

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:03.480
at a particular speed and
moved this limb and that limb,

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:07.743
but by fractionating it something
is most definitely lost.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:12.480
So, if someone wanted to,
let's say, get in better touch

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:14.100
with their body, in quotes,

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:18.753
in order to explore the
infinite space that is movement,

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:21.600
how might they begin to approach that?

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:23.850
Is it does it begin with an awareness,

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:25.920
with practice, or both?

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:27.067
- It begins with education.

00:18:27.067 --> 00:18:31.590
You know, that's probably the
most stable point of entry,

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:34.548
awareness to some something as a concept

00:18:34.548 --> 00:18:38.520
that it is a concept,
that there is validity,

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:41.460
or because sometimes people look for that

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.660
to looking at this
entity, this open entity.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:49.380
And that's part of the reason
why answering questions

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:53.610
is not something I can do

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:55.560
or even attempt to do.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:59.880
I believe in the power of
the non-complete process,

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:03.000
like making this table,

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.850
but living something undone,

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:08.340
not perfecting the product, why?

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:11.760
Because it offers some kind of
a dynamic nature of evolution

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:14.073
that naturally unravels from it.

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:19.740
Almost like sometimes I do it,

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:22.720
I count reps and I'll only count to nine

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.880
'cause it tends to leave
people in the count

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.790
and it keeps going instead
of giving them the 10.

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:31.440
- Everyone wants to end on 10. [laughs]

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:36.000
- Yeah, which is because of
the decimal system, et cetera.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:40.140
So, all kinds of things
like that is also important

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:42.780
with the movement idea is to discuss,

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:46.050
to examine, to look, to taste, to try,

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:47.850
but then also not to try to capture

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:51.600
because if you like the
invisible loop of Hoffstadt.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:54.510
If you look at it too closely, it's gone,

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:56.190
but if you look away,

00:19:56.190 --> 00:19:59.670
it functions and exists
just like us very powerfully

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:04.670
and obviously gives us the
experiences that we have.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:07.830
So, when people enter movement practice,

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:09.750
it is about education,

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:13.620
bringing some awareness to
the fact that they are living

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:16.590
in a body, that they are living in motion,

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:19.470
that their mind is a type of movement,

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:22.260
that their life is a type of movement.

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:26.370
Bringing attention to the
movement of the emotions as well.

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.320
Bringing just attention to the fact

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:30.810
that things are in motion.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:35.810
The Heraclitus panta rhei, all in flux,

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:42.380
nothing stops besides something
that is the background of it

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:44.160
and allows it to express,

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.230
and this is the beauty of things.

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:48.953
And this for me is the movement practice

00:20:48.953 --> 00:20:52.680
is this examination and bringing
this awareness into things.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:56.343
As we sit now here, I'm
also aware of my body,

00:20:58.530 --> 00:21:01.140
I'm also aware of the way
that things make me feel,

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.480
the way that your face
is communicating to me.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:07.570
And I'm not just in some limited

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:11.370
and very verbal, overly verbal state

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:14.073
because it misses a lot
of the beautiful flux.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.960
- I'm going to inject some
or project some ideas,

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:22.833
and perhaps you would tell
me if they're ridiculous,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:25.503
potentially useful, or useful.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.130
As I understand what
we're talking about now

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:32.100
and what we've discussed
earlier is that movement

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:36.030
can and should be incorporated
into one's entire life.

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:36.863
I've even heard you say

00:21:36.863 --> 00:21:38.730
that even before getting
out of bed in the morning,

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:40.860
one can experience movement,

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:43.170
and it doesn't necessarily
have to be of the intimate kind

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:46.530
with somebody else, it's
it can be paying attention

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:47.910
to the rhythm of one's breath,

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:49.290
or how you get out of the bed.

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:52.410
Or actually, in anticipation
of you arriving here today,

00:21:52.410 --> 00:21:54.454
I noticed that as I was
going up and down the stairs

00:21:54.454 --> 00:21:58.920
in this house, that I was injecting

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:00.030
a little bit of playfulness

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.460
in the way that I might
have many, many decades ago,

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:04.830
but haven't for a very long time.

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:07.500
And I asked myself whether or not

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:09.720
that's what Ido is referring to

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:12.750
when he talks about
threading this body awareness

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:13.980
throughout the day.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.680
As opposed to, but of course not exclusive

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:19.140
from just saying I have 45 minutes,

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.090
I'm going to do movement practice

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:22.920
before I shower and have some dinner.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:24.450
Right, I have to imagine both are helpful,

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:25.920
but in terms of moving through the day

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:27.543
and having bodily awareness,

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:30.450
clearly there are an
infinite number of ways

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:31.283
one could do that,

00:22:31.283 --> 00:22:32.580
maybe you could just share a few.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.070
You mentioned, I mean,
one could pay attention

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:37.920
to their breath, could
pay attention to posture.

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:40.620
And this notion of play
is a very attractive

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:43.110
or as we say in science,
it's a sticky concept,

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:45.540
a concept that kind of draws one in.

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:48.360
Maybe if you would, could you
share with us just some ideas

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:49.500
to get people thinking about

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:51.150
or maybe even incorporating

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:53.430
movement practice into their day,

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:56.640
and maybe even touch on the potential role

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:58.320
of play or playfulness?

00:22:58.320 --> 00:22:59.153
- Okay.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:04.980
Yeah, those are some good directions.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:09.141
I think one thing is this
what you call wordlessness.

00:23:09.141 --> 00:23:14.141
I have been recommending to
people non-verbal experiences

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:16.890
and the awareness of the body,

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:20.730
which is not really the awareness
of the body, as you know,

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:23.370
not purely or not fully.

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:28.370
The awareness of motion is
a very good way to start

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:31.230
to bring awareness to that layer.

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:33.360
And that layer will start to get clarified

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:35.157
more and more and more,
the more you practice.

00:23:35.157 --> 00:23:37.830
And then, it will enable for most people,

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:42.000
a safe haven away from many
states and difficulties,

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:46.310
and will unlock a lot
of potential attributes,

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:51.363
and strength, and freshness,
and a lot of beautiful things.

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:55.650
Really one of the really perspectives

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:58.980
about who we are comes from a person

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:00.330
who influenced my thinking a lot,

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.030
Moshe Feldenkrais, the
late Moshe Feldenkrais.

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:07.080
And he talks about the body
as the core three elements,

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:08.463
the core nervous system,

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:14.810
two is the mechanical system
of muscle, skeleton, et cetera,

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.280
and the third is the environment,

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:20.220
which is a unique way to look at it.

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.110
And he talks about how the nervous system

00:24:22.110 --> 00:24:26.310
is both receiving
information from the outside

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:27.270
and from the inside.

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:29.130
And in the first years of life,

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:33.420
you work a lot on differentiating those,

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:36.390
what is me and what is not me?

00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:39.480
And I think movement,
when you feel movement,

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:42.240
you feel the movement of the outside

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:45.450
that is, of course, arriving
to you and receiving this,

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:47.337
and also your own internal movement.

00:24:47.337 --> 00:24:49.713
And the same can be said for stillness.

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:54.390
So, bringing the attention
into those layers,

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:55.560
it's a tricky thing.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.830
It's one of those elusive
things to look at,

00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:02.760
but it's definitely of huge
benefit to start to train it,

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:07.590
start to practice it, to
feel not our thoughts,

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:10.470
not necessarily our body,

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.720
but to start to recognize

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:15.900
the dynamic nature, the flux, the motion,

00:25:15.900 --> 00:25:17.733
and it occurs in all these layers.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:22.860
So, you will need to find
it in multiple locations

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:27.240
before you start to more
and more make it your own,

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:28.563
make it really yours.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:32.010
How, for example,

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:34.266
simple, pragmatic things
I used to do this,

00:25:34.266 --> 00:25:37.169
I spent some time in Hong Kong,

00:25:37.169 --> 00:25:39.480
I would need to get my practice in,

00:25:39.480 --> 00:25:43.680
but I'm really turned
off from commercial gyms

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:46.740
and there is not a lot of
nature accessible there

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:48.900
so I would just strap on my bag

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:50.910
and I would walk the streets of Hong Kong,

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:52.290
which are very crowded

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:55.740
and then I would try to
avoid touching anyone.

00:25:55.740 --> 00:25:57.750
And it would be like two hours

00:25:57.750 --> 00:25:59.850
of just like moving, involved,

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:01.470
fully involved, fully in my body,

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:04.380
and experiencing beautiful things,

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:06.990
and enjoying and developing myself as well

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:08.250
in all kinds of scenarios,

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:11.550
up and down in the escalators and off.

00:26:11.550 --> 00:26:16.550
So, this is an example
of a way to practice.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:19.350
And then, the way that we are sitting

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:21.390
like these chairs, for example,

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:22.950
our chairs are not very dynamic,

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:24.480
but there is rocking chairs, right?

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:27.810
And this is something I
recommend for a lot of kids

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:30.930
like in schools, I used
to rock on the chair,

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:32.294
which is very common.

00:26:32.294 --> 00:26:33.360
I'm sure-
- I used to put my skateboard

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:35.190
underneath my chair and
roll it back and forth,

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:37.590
and the teacher would tell me to stop.

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:39.090
And I would just slowly little by little

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:40.710
trying to get the most
subtle movement I could

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:42.360
without them telling me

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:44.430
they were going to take it away, or try.

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:47.250
- Which is probably
horrible, horrible advice

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:50.040
and instruction just like sit up straight

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:51.810
and chew with your mouth closed

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:56.220
because they remove a
lot of the self-education

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:57.780
and a lot of the self-development

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:00.450
and the practical discoveries
that are necessary

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:03.420
and even will damage focus

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:07.353
and thinking processes in some ways.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:12.730
So, for example, I would make
the chairs even more mobile

00:27:14.187 --> 00:27:16.740
and I would support more motion,

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:19.200
and then I would be able
to bring attention there,

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:22.260
but I would also be able to
bring attention away from it

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:23.700
into other things.

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:27.780
And it keeps refreshing me
so I don't become stale,

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:29.220
the water doesn't stand,

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:30.957
this is the beauty of movement.

00:27:30.957 --> 00:27:33.360
So, you can focus for long periods of time

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:35.100
and do incredible things with the mind,

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:37.680
with focus, with awareness, attention.

00:27:37.680 --> 00:27:40.260
And it's like we're skin in the game,

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:43.020
so I'm not talking as some meditator

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:46.650
and he's describing the
act of being very focused,

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:49.320
but then I put a stick on
the edge of his fingers

00:27:49.320 --> 00:27:50.520
and I tell him, "Balance it."

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:52.860
And everyone can do it for 10 seconds

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:55.530
and I tell him, "Okay,
now hold it 10 minutes."

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:59.850
And you see that the skill has,
he has no skin in the game.

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:02.880
It wasn't developed in various scenarios,

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:05.440
but so there is a delusion
that start to develop

00:28:06.397 --> 00:28:11.190
and that's how movement
keeps me very honest

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:14.670
and humble in the way that I view humility

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:17.760
and in a way that protects me

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:22.760
and keeps me, yeah, it keeps me fresh.

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:27.840
- I loved the example of moving
through the crowded street

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:29.130
with a backpack because of the way

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:30.780
in which it's completely adaptive

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:32.910
to the situation you happened to be in

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:36.480
and highlights the fact
that one doesn't need a gym

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:39.720
or any specific scenario,

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:41.280
although we will certainly touch

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:43.830
on ideal learning
circumstances for movement

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:47.640
and some of the work that
you're doing, of course.

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:50.550
- The less of your own personal practice,

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:53.610
and understanding, and
knowledge you've done,

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:55.053
the more toys you need.

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:59.193
The more you've really worked on yourself,

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:01.800
the more high-tech you are.

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:03.810
The more low-tech are your tools,

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:05.280
the more high-tech you are.

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:08.760
And this is the most advanced technology

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:10.680
by far on this planet.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:13.969
With all the advancement, it
doesn't even start to scratch,

00:29:13.969 --> 00:29:14.820
and you know it,

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:17.580
from the way that we understand the eyes

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:21.663
all the way to with all the
respect to the Boston Robotics,

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:24.780
a 5-year-old motion-

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:26.940
- Yeah, yeah.
- Movements.

00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:30.210
Or animal motion with very underdeveloped,

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:33.090
still relatively to us as systems.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:37.050
So, important to remind ourselves.

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:40.200
- A lot can be done with
the body and gravity

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:41.460
as I-
- Floor, a piece of floor,

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:45.630
a piece of wall, a corner of a
room is a beautiful scenario,

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:50.340
which you can become
discover in and play in.

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:53.190
And, but we are not so developed

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:55.050
so we don't see those options.

00:29:55.050 --> 00:29:56.970
And this is something
that I try to stimulate,

00:29:56.970 --> 00:29:59.790
and that's why I made it a point to avoid

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:02.730
any of the big sponsorship

00:30:02.730 --> 00:30:05.040
and high-tech tools.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:08.100
And I on point brought a stick

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:10.620
into big conventions

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:15.620
and/or sometimes I use
a shirt with holes in it

00:30:15.633 --> 00:30:18.300
that just like a used
shirt as a point to make

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:22.535
when I'm addressing a crowd to keep things

00:30:22.535 --> 00:30:26.370
where it's important and it's important,

00:30:26.370 --> 00:30:30.120
we are important, and our
experience is important.

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:32.580
And we have to be very careful,

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:36.420
these habits and these directions,

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:38.760
they come from many times good intention,

00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:42.150
but they are the devil many times,

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:43.650
they turn into the devil,

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:46.200
just like our technology nowadays

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:48.990
and what is happening with
people and with depression,

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:51.663
with meaning, meaninglessness,

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:55.470
also with the body in
various perspectives,

00:30:55.470 --> 00:31:00.180
or even I will also flip it
into high performance sports

00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:02.520
and their price.

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:04.710
Because for me, this is
not a movement practice,

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:07.830
it erases the person in the center of it.

00:31:07.830 --> 00:31:12.830
And then came places like
skateboarding or break dancing

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:15.270
where somebody with a disability

00:31:15.270 --> 00:31:17.010
becomes the best in the world,

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.200
turns it into the biggest advantage,

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:22.380
but you would never be
accepted into gymnastics class,

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:24.150
and I love that.

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:25.830
And that change

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:30.830
to place change in the
center, it's important.

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:33.873
- You touched on mention of a few sports,

00:31:34.950 --> 00:31:36.180
maybe it was Charles Poliquin,

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:40.230
or maybe it was another
trainer that I heard once say

00:31:40.230 --> 00:31:44.340
that, "For kids, one of the
worst things they can do

00:31:44.340 --> 00:31:47.073
is overspecialize in a particular sport."

00:31:47.073 --> 00:31:51.570
The idea being that it leads
to improvements in performance

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:52.950
in a very narrow domain,

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:55.450
but they raised the idea

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:58.920
that it could perhaps also constrains

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:00.210
the development of the nervous system,

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:02.686
such that certain emotional states,

00:32:02.686 --> 00:32:06.300
certain intellectual abilities
will forever be shut off

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:09.300
because of the intense plasticity
that occurs early in life.

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:11.700
The more I learn from you,

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:13.290
the more I'm thinking that that statement

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:15.390
really should be extended to all of life.

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:18.150
And I love to remind people

00:32:18.150 --> 00:32:20.400
because I started off as a
developmental neurobiologist,

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:22.290
that development doesn't start and end,

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:24.180
you don't have childhood and adulthood.

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:25.950
Our life is one long developmental arc

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:28.710
from birth until death,
however long that might be.

00:32:28.710 --> 00:32:32.820
So, if one is going to be anti-specialist,

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.700
maybe even we call that a generalist,

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:36.690
what does that look like?

00:32:36.690 --> 00:32:39.630
What are the different
domains of movement practice?

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:44.630
And as I asked this, I
realized I am in serious danger

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:48.300
of fractionating movement
into a list of words

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:52.083
like strength, and speed, and
explosiveness, and suppleness,

00:32:52.083 --> 00:32:54.480
a word that I've heard you use before.

00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:55.530
And yet, I think for most people

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:58.503
because we think in words, often,

00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:01.560
some of those categories can be useful.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:03.630
So, let's say I was going to
embark on a movement practice

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:06.510
or a child was going to
embark on a movement practice

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:08.400
and either throughout the day

00:33:08.400 --> 00:33:10.920
or for a dedicated period of time,

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:12.780
what are the sorts of
categories of movement

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:14.790
that I might want to think about,

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:17.100
ballistic movement, smooth movement?

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:19.080
Maybe you could just enrich us

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:21.930
with some of the landscape around that.

00:33:21.930 --> 00:33:24.960
- Okay, first I'll address the first part

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:26.670
that you mentioned.

00:33:26.670 --> 00:33:31.350
And I've learned from
you about certain changes

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:35.310
in the way that things
develop later in life

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:36.990
versus earlier in life.

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:38.550
And you're right, it is something

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:40.137
that Charles Poliquin also mentioned

00:33:40.137 --> 00:33:42.933
and I learned from back in
the day as well from him,

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:47.850
which can seem dark a
bit and kind of hopeless,

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:52.293
but then you should go beyond that.

00:33:53.820 --> 00:33:58.820
One thing that does seem to appear

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:01.710
for me when I look around

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:05.913
is these the concepts of unique postures.

00:34:08.160 --> 00:34:11.403
And I think this is true
for postures of thought,

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:16.140
emotional postures, and movement postures.

00:34:16.140 --> 00:34:18.780
Truly, earlier in life,

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:21.850
we are creating these unique postures

00:34:22.770 --> 00:34:26.250
and they get into these drawers

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:29.910
or like language letters.

00:34:29.910 --> 00:34:33.660
Later in life, the process moves more

00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:36.390
towards integration of
these unique postures

00:34:36.390 --> 00:34:39.180
into all different organizations.

00:34:39.180 --> 00:34:41.880
The beauty of it is that you
can use very few postures

00:34:41.880 --> 00:34:44.280
to create many possibilities,

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:47.294
just like a lib needs
a search for a language

00:34:47.294 --> 00:34:52.140
that contain one symbol only versus two,

00:34:52.140 --> 00:34:53.313
which he discovered.

00:34:56.186 --> 00:35:01.186
And this is something that is often seen,

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:04.950
like you take someone who
moves in a certain way

00:35:04.950 --> 00:35:08.250
and you teach them all these
new sports or techniques,

00:35:08.250 --> 00:35:11.520
but essentially if you look
deeply and you're sensitive,

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:14.070
you see it's the same postures

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:17.043
that he will have to work
with till the end of his life,

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:20.610
the same thinking postures.

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:24.300
And this is really problematic,

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:28.050
where we are not freeing

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:32.013
the mind beyond this,

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:37.860
how would I say, a scaffolding of thinking

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:40.350
and we are actually
letting go of the content.

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:43.260
We get more and more focused

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:48.260
on the way of thinking

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:50.340
versus the thinking itself,

00:35:50.340 --> 00:35:54.630
or habitual ways and forms of thinking,

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:56.253
associated thinking, et cetera.

00:35:57.270 --> 00:35:58.650
And emotionally the same,

00:35:58.650 --> 00:36:01.140
we are constructing
these emotional postures

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:03.360
and then we have to go
through the rest of our lives

00:36:03.360 --> 00:36:05.040
working with that.

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.620
So, this is the dark side, right?

00:36:07.620 --> 00:36:11.910
But of course, there are
always possibilities,

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:16.590
both I think invading this early system,

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:21.590
to some extent, even if it's
5%, or 7%, or whatever percent,

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:24.930
and also on the freeing yourself

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:27.843
of going beyond all postures period.

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:30.960
Working with the postures you have,

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:35.340
but towards a postureless
way of doing things.

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:38.400
So, this is something interesting to work

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:40.470
when people work with movements,

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:44.520
but finally are able to go into movement

00:36:44.520 --> 00:36:46.260
and this magic starts to happen,

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:50.283
and then the techniques fall
apart and something appears,

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:55.983
and it's a face change,
it's a transformation,

00:36:57.540 --> 00:36:59.400
it's a binary moment.

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:00.708
There is a jump there for sure,

00:37:00.708 --> 00:37:05.670
and it's very rare to see both
in thinking, and emotionally,

00:37:05.670 --> 00:37:08.337
and other ways, we have many names for it.

00:37:08.337 --> 00:37:10.650
And some talk about enlightenment

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:13.347
and some talk about all kinds
of processes related to it.

00:37:13.347 --> 00:37:14.940
And I think most of them

00:37:14.940 --> 00:37:18.690
are shadows of the sun,

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:20.703
but it's not the sun itself really.

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:25.560
And then, talking about ways
of thinking about movement,

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:30.000
this is where I use something
I called my slice and dice

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.280
because of the problem of using words,

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:33.810
and definitions, and categories,

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:36.690
I try to create a lot of them

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:40.770
and I write them on the paper
and then I crumble them,

00:37:40.770 --> 00:37:44.910
throw them into the bin, and
I keep doing it all my life.

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:47.940
The writing them down
and the geeking on it

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:51.093
is very important, also
very important to let it go.

00:37:52.260 --> 00:37:57.090
I tell people what you
forgot is not the same,

00:37:57.090 --> 00:37:59.970
forgetting is not the
same as never knowing it.

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:03.450
The crumbling and throwing
away is a form of forgetting,

00:38:03.450 --> 00:38:05.740
but it leaves some kind of a homeopathic

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:10.050
trace behind.

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:12.750
So, let's take some slice and dice,

00:38:12.750 --> 00:38:14.490
and try to look at it.

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:18.963
Here is a physical one,
contraction, relaxation.

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:21.900
That's a spectrum,

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:24.870
and pretty much everything
falls on this spectrum.

00:38:24.870 --> 00:38:28.470
Also, in terms of analyzing
a person or yourself,

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:31.800
you can tell me if you
feel closer to this side

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:33.930
or closer to that side,

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:36.240
and then it allows you to
examine your practices.

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:40.980
How many of the practices are
moving you towards balance

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:43.050
and how many are it's your addiction

00:38:43.050 --> 00:38:46.143
of just doing what you're
good at versus what you need?

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:50.253
Here is another example, physical culture.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:53.940
So, we have the dense realm

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:57.720
working with internal concepts

00:38:57.720 --> 00:39:01.233
and expressing them, abstract
concepts, expression.

00:39:03.390 --> 00:39:06.000
Second perspective, the Marshall concept,

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:07.830
but not in the sense of just fighting,

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:11.803
but also partnering,
working with another person,

00:39:11.803 --> 00:39:15.420
a dynamic entity that is
communicating with you.

00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:17.460
The third one is I call the elements,

00:39:17.460 --> 00:39:19.533
working with the environment.

00:39:21.510 --> 00:39:25.860
The next one is a somatic
one, is the internal practice.

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:27.930
And of course, there are all gray zones,

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:31.290
and another one is object manipulatory,

00:39:31.290 --> 00:39:33.150
which you can think of it
also as the environment,

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:35.430
but it's more small objects,

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:38.040
heavy objects, many objects, few objects.

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:40.710
And then you can look
at this way of thinking,

00:39:40.710 --> 00:39:43.740
and you can say, "Oh, I
have many of my practices

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:45.390
in this direction, but not,"

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:47.790
and you can draw it for yourself.

00:39:47.790 --> 00:39:49.410
So, that's another perspective,

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:53.100
and this way I use dozens of perspectives.

00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:55.140
And with the years,

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:57.720
it gives people a sense
of where they want to go,

00:39:57.720 --> 00:39:58.950
how they want to do it,

00:39:58.950 --> 00:40:00.480
and what they need to address

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:02.910
versus what they like
to address, et cetera.

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:03.743
Is it helpful?

00:40:03.743 --> 00:40:06.843
- Very helpful, those different
bins are very helpful.

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:10.260
I really appreciate that you mentioned

00:40:10.260 --> 00:40:12.930
that people will often
practice what they're good at

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:14.700
as opposed to what they need.

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:18.450
In gym culture, we refer to this

00:40:18.450 --> 00:40:22.140
as the guy that always skips
leg day type of person, right?

00:40:22.140 --> 00:40:23.910
You know, big upper body, skinny legs,

00:40:23.910 --> 00:40:25.350
or you you'll see people

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:27.360
that have these enormous thick torsos

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:28.650
and they're bench pressing all day,

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:31.560
but they clearly need to pull on an object

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:33.420
every once in a while
to create some balance,

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:37.680
but they don't do it because
they, for whatever reason,

00:40:37.680 --> 00:40:39.540
they have an obsession with moving

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:42.930
greater and greater poundage
or something like that,

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:45.810
which in certain sports like power lifting

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:47.610
or aesthetics aren't the goal,

00:40:47.610 --> 00:40:49.860
and it's simply to push
more weight off one's chest

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:50.693
that you could imagine

00:40:50.693 --> 00:40:52.260
that there's something beneficial there.

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:55.920
However, I think that
it's really important

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:59.970
in intellectual endeavors
and in movement endeavors,

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:00.960
if I understand correctly,

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:03.900
to bring oneself to a
place of real challenge

00:41:03.900 --> 00:41:05.520
on a regular basis.

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:06.780
In fact, earlier today,

00:41:06.780 --> 00:41:08.250
I was in a state of constant challenge

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:09.960
'cause it was all new to me.

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:12.480
And as much as I told
myself, "Beginners' mind,

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:14.040
beginners' mind, beginners' mind,"

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:17.250
it's hard, I confess,
to not want to do well,

00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:18.510
to perform well, right?

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:21.180
And I think that's a
natural and healthy thing.

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:22.950
Yet-
- Not only natural,

00:41:22.950 --> 00:41:24.870
it is necessary,

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:28.230
but I want you to keep it on that side

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:31.350
and to bring something to balance it.

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:35.880
If there is not this challenge,
the process will not work.

00:41:35.880 --> 00:41:37.470
It has to be this scale.

00:41:37.470 --> 00:41:40.260
And you're talking about
scales of pain, pleasure,

00:41:40.260 --> 00:41:42.930
and this is another scale.

00:41:42.930 --> 00:41:46.380
And this discomfort again is necessary

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:49.920
and should be recognized as,
"I'm in the right place."

00:41:49.920 --> 00:41:54.630
When it becomes too high
and I'm unable to resolve,

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:56.313
to make any progress,

00:41:57.810 --> 00:42:00.903
I went overboard, but
when it's not present,

00:42:01.740 --> 00:42:03.420
I don't do nothing here.

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:05.340
Nothing that I'm truly interested in,

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:08.460
I'm just gratifying myself, wankery.

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:11.223
It's in essence,

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:15.723
it's not about searching
for the discomfort,

00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:17.673
but it's a marker.

00:42:19.200 --> 00:42:23.760
And I think the question

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:25.533
should be who am I serving?

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:29.703
'Cause people do not serve
themselves, in essence,

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:33.941
they serve part, parts of it,

00:42:33.941 --> 00:42:37.830
some kind of a fraction of themselves.

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:41.610
And this separation of
oneself from oneself,

00:42:41.610 --> 00:42:45.993
and this is also a result of
the practice, a good practice.

00:42:47.370 --> 00:42:50.580
I think maybe the biggest gift I received

00:42:50.580 --> 00:42:54.210
from the practice is I can say

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:58.170
although it will take
maybe a certain context,

00:42:58.170 --> 00:42:59.343
I am not my friend.

00:43:01.530 --> 00:43:02.910
At times I am,

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:05.130
but many times I am not my friend.

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:07.200
And by creating this separation,

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:10.560
I can assume a certain stability

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:13.020
in the face of everything

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:16.350
all the way up to our
own mortality and death,

00:43:16.350 --> 00:43:19.713
which is, and maybe beyond, who knows?

00:43:20.580 --> 00:43:24.210
- Yeah, it was a striking
moment for me earlier today

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:25.800
when I was really challenged

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:28.980
with one of the practices we were doing,

00:43:28.980 --> 00:43:32.370
and you said, "This is
exactly what I experienced

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:35.190
this morning, Andrew,"
that's what you said.

00:43:35.190 --> 00:43:37.440
And I couldn't imagine that
you were having challenges

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:38.970
doing what I was attempting to do.

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:39.803
And of course, you were,

00:43:39.803 --> 00:43:41.250
and I believe what you were referring to

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:43.770
is that you had put yourself at that edge

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:46.890
earlier in the day in which
you were making failures,

00:43:46.890 --> 00:43:48.630
you were failing to execute

00:43:48.630 --> 00:43:51.930
the way that you were
attempting to execute movement.

00:43:51.930 --> 00:43:54.390
I should just to inject some neuroscience

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:56.760
and neuroplasticity there,
I can't help myself,

00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:59.070
it is what I do after all.

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:01.860
There are beautiful data
in animals and humans

00:44:01.860 --> 00:44:05.160
showing that in the seconds and minutes

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:09.210
after a failed attempt at a
motor execution of something,

00:44:09.210 --> 00:44:11.730
the forebrain is in a
heightened state of focus.

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:14.460
And when you hear it,
suddenly makes perfect sense,

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:16.710
of course, why would the
nervous system change

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:18.900
unless it got a cue to change?

00:44:18.900 --> 00:44:23.310
And the cue almost always comes
in the form of frustration,

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:26.730
the [imitates buzzer] or
as we said earlier, nah,

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:28.350
the nah signal
[Ido laughing]

00:44:28.350 --> 00:44:31.920
is the one that preps you
to extract more learning

00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:33.690
from the subsequent trials.

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:35.520
And yet, for a lot of people,

00:44:35.520 --> 00:44:38.460
they feel that ugh,
that failure to execute,

00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:42.120
or even to approximate execution

00:44:42.120 --> 00:44:46.500
and they feel and experience
that ah negative signal,

00:44:46.500 --> 00:44:49.020
and they lean out of the practice,

00:44:49.020 --> 00:44:52.590
they start to depart either
mentally or physically or both.

00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:55.920
And if there's anything I
think that perhaps we can offer

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:57.510
is this understanding that that edge

00:44:57.510 --> 00:44:58.380
as some people call it

00:44:58.380 --> 00:45:01.230
or that failures aren't just necessary,

00:45:01.230 --> 00:45:03.750
they are part of the learning process,

00:45:03.750 --> 00:45:07.710
they are the entry gate
to neuroplasticity.

00:45:07.710 --> 00:45:11.170
- Yes, contextualizing
or recontextualizing

00:45:12.300 --> 00:45:15.100
that sensation is
something I work a lot with

00:45:15.100 --> 00:45:16.830
and I just remind people

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:19.020
and I also reminded to myself,

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:20.670
and if it wasn't difficult

00:45:20.670 --> 00:45:23.700
and we didn't need to
redo it again and again,

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:27.270
we wouldn't be again
on this correct scale,

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:31.410
which is dynamic and moving
just like rolling downhill.

00:45:31.410 --> 00:45:35.700
So, there is definitely a
necessity to succeed, to orient,

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:39.240
there is certain aspects
that you want to achieve,

00:45:39.240 --> 00:45:43.680
but then there is also
the letting go of it

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:45.940
and the deambitioning of it.

00:45:45.940 --> 00:45:49.320
And within that tension,

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:51.480
the plus and the minus comes movement,

00:45:51.480 --> 00:45:52.857
and that that's how I did it.

00:45:52.857 --> 00:45:55.140
And again, if I stretch it too far away,

00:45:55.140 --> 00:45:57.227
or if I increase one of them too much,

00:45:57.227 --> 00:45:59.130
then I would have some issues,

00:45:59.130 --> 00:46:01.710
but you will with practice

00:46:01.710 --> 00:46:06.710
learn to recognize the
optimal point of progression.

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:09.547
Of course, it takes many years

00:46:09.547 --> 00:46:14.250
and a lot of play and
exposure to get a sense of it,

00:46:14.250 --> 00:46:18.090
regardless of the layer
in which it is applied.

00:46:18.090 --> 00:46:22.260
So, I'm sure in your field
and in your pursuits,

00:46:22.260 --> 00:46:26.190
you are already aware of it
and applying it in your life,

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:29.130
talking about focus, talking
about ways of thinking,

00:46:29.130 --> 00:46:30.960
creativity, et cetera.

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:34.080
But then it's enough that I
pull into another perspective

00:46:34.080 --> 00:46:36.450
and you will see that
people are specialists,

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:40.562
and then they don't have
really the real essence

00:46:40.562 --> 00:46:42.150
of the concept.

00:46:42.150 --> 00:46:45.780
It's not theirs, it's
applied specifically.

00:46:45.780 --> 00:46:48.150
The one who changes all the time

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:49.770
gets the general component

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:51.923
because what appears
when everything changes?

00:46:51.923 --> 00:46:54.450
That is that new entity.

00:46:54.450 --> 00:46:58.110
Everything changes, something stays.

00:46:58.110 --> 00:46:59.190
That's what we want to get,

00:46:59.190 --> 00:47:01.743
this concept and this understanding.

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:04.320
- I've heard the statement before,

00:47:04.320 --> 00:47:06.360
we are just a meat vehicle, right?

00:47:06.360 --> 00:47:08.460
We're just a sack of cells

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:11.520
and it's I truly despise that statement

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:12.900
because, first of all,

00:47:12.900 --> 00:47:16.680
it deprives us of all
meaning in of our lives.

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:20.130
And we can go down the route of philosophy

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:22.260
as to whether or not
there's meaning or not,

00:47:22.260 --> 00:47:25.380
but more importantly, it
divorces us from the idea

00:47:25.380 --> 00:47:27.150
that the body and brain are interconnected

00:47:27.150 --> 00:47:30.990
and have at least equal
value at any one moment

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:32.190
that they're informing each other.

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:35.790
Emotions inform movement,
movement informs emotions.

00:47:35.790 --> 00:47:37.223
One thing that I've heard you say before,

00:47:37.223 --> 00:47:40.980
and I'd really love to
hear you embellish on

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:43.230
is this important principle

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:45.480
that human beings are truly unique

00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:48.420
in terms of the enormous
range of movements

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:49.533
that we can perform.

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:52.410
And yet, we are excellent,

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:54.720
maybe superior to all other species

00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:57.390
at certain types of movement.

00:47:57.390 --> 00:48:00.810
The one that comes to mind is
walking, strides, striding.

00:48:00.810 --> 00:48:05.520
So, maybe we could just explore that idea

00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:08.820
because obviously a cheetah is very fast,

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:11.010
the gibbon seems to have
a lot of proficiency

00:48:11.010 --> 00:48:12.960
at grabbing and swinging from branches,

00:48:14.490 --> 00:48:17.400
but human beings perform an enormous

00:48:17.400 --> 00:48:19.980
or can potentially perform an
enormous array of movements.

00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:21.360
Do you think all human beings

00:48:21.360 --> 00:48:24.180
are potentially able to explore

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:25.980
all the different types of movement?

00:48:27.210 --> 00:48:29.700
And if so, how does one approach that?

00:48:29.700 --> 00:48:33.120
So, basically what I'm doing
is I'm tabling a concept,

00:48:33.120 --> 00:48:35.430
which is not range of motion,

00:48:35.430 --> 00:48:38.610
right, for the gym rats,
discard with range of motion,

00:48:38.610 --> 00:48:42.063
I'm talking about the
variety of movements.

00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:47.370
- First, it's not important what I think,

00:48:47.370 --> 00:48:49.530
if it's possible or not possible,

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:53.520
or if it's even possible for
you or not possible for you,

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:58.520
what is important is what
you truly want to do,

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:00.480
what you truly are after.

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:02.670
And it's important for
me because many times,

00:49:02.670 --> 00:49:06.693
this way of thinking about
things is already limited.

00:49:07.890 --> 00:49:10.710
I like to say

00:49:10.710 --> 00:49:14.223
a man doesn't go to the ocean
to empty it with a spoon.

00:49:15.360 --> 00:49:20.360
A lot of the types of dressing
up of the concepts nowadays

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:22.410
is trying to fit an elephant

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:25.503
into the hole in the needle, yeah?

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:29.463
Like for example, the concept of practice,

00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:34.533
and then our lives, as if we have a life.

00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:41.370
We have some kind of
a stream of behaviors,

00:49:41.370 --> 00:49:45.030
we have there is an argument
of free will, et cetera.

00:49:45.030 --> 00:49:49.440
There is a multiplicity,
definitely a man is a legion,

00:49:49.440 --> 00:49:52.173
that's the real meaning of that phrase.

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:55.830
One day you wake up like this,

00:49:55.830 --> 00:49:58.320
I say, "Andrew, let's
meet tomorrow at 7:00 am,"

00:49:58.320 --> 00:50:00.570
but I don't know who's
going to wake up tomorrow.

00:50:00.570 --> 00:50:02.403
And then you send me a text message,

00:50:03.307 --> 00:50:06.360
"Oh, I'm feeling off,"

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:09.540
right at 6:55 and go back to sleep.

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:12.270
So, examining that and seeing that

00:50:12.270 --> 00:50:14.190
I think frees you up eventually

00:50:14.190 --> 00:50:17.613
and start to orient you
in a better direction.

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:22.350
So, what do you want to do and what,

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:25.530
but in the orientation of
also what you need to do,

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:29.010
what you sense, and
what you are developing

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:32.850
as an evolutionary direction for you,

00:50:32.850 --> 00:50:34.443
this is the important bit.

00:50:35.340 --> 00:50:37.380
Is it possible for everyone to engage

00:50:37.380 --> 00:50:39.390
in certain specific physical movement?

00:50:39.390 --> 00:50:41.880
For example, in Scandinavian countries,

00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:43.893
the squat is not very approachable,

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:48.780
it's very difficult.

00:50:48.780 --> 00:50:51.690
They're more built for
dragging heavy things

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:55.410
and also in this climate, I guess it,

00:50:55.410 --> 00:50:57.330
it makes less sense to squat

00:50:57.330 --> 00:50:59.430
and 'cause you're going to freeze there.

00:50:59.430 --> 00:51:03.870
So, this is and then you see
the squat in warm climates

00:51:03.870 --> 00:51:07.050
and it's like so open and accessible.

00:51:07.050 --> 00:51:09.693
They're very good deadlifters usually,

00:51:10.830 --> 00:51:13.082
not good squatters and the-

00:51:13.082 --> 00:51:14.650
- They want to get away from the ground.

00:51:14.650 --> 00:51:18.810
- Yeah, the shallow hip socket,
which allows one activity,

00:51:18.810 --> 00:51:21.210
but then the stability
of the deep hip socket

00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:23.613
architecture of the hip,

00:51:24.600 --> 00:51:28.380
the femur heads, the Q
angles, the shapes, et cetera.

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:31.290
So, we are all unique and
there are certain elements,

00:51:31.290 --> 00:51:33.450
which like, for example,
my squat challenge

00:51:33.450 --> 00:51:36.300
is like for most people

00:51:36.300 --> 00:51:37.150
there is something there, but-

00:51:37.150 --> 00:51:39.720
- Could you remind people
what the squat challenge is?

00:51:39.720 --> 00:51:43.590
- The squat was my attempt to bring

00:51:43.590 --> 00:51:48.183
a new, fresh state of
mind into the word squat,

00:51:49.080 --> 00:51:51.270
not as a strength element,

00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:55.590
but it's a fundamental
resting position really.

00:51:55.590 --> 00:51:58.050
Actually, should be one
of the most abundant ones,

00:51:58.050 --> 00:52:00.930
we replaced it with sitting,

00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:02.580
which is not really doesn't work well

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:04.530
if you're in a natural environment,

00:52:04.530 --> 00:52:05.700
it's not very comfortable

00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:07.500
actually to sit for long periods of time,

00:52:07.500 --> 00:52:09.120
rocks and different terrains

00:52:09.120 --> 00:52:13.350
so you end up lying down,
standing, and squatting a lot.

00:52:13.350 --> 00:52:15.630
Also, when you're moving low and dynamic,

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:17.460
like even collecting berries,

00:52:17.460 --> 00:52:20.910
the squat is much more dynamic and open.

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:22.980
And then elimination is happening there,

00:52:22.980 --> 00:52:24.720
so it's like it's such a fundamental thing

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:26.880
and we totally eliminated it.

00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:28.530
We eliminated many other things,

00:52:28.530 --> 00:52:32.040
overhead movements, behind the back,

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:34.080
all kinds of back realm

00:52:34.080 --> 00:52:36.780
what I call the back
realm is totally absent

00:52:36.780 --> 00:52:39.210
in people's awareness.

00:52:39.210 --> 00:52:41.430
So, that was my attempt to
bring it back into people,

00:52:41.430 --> 00:52:44.340
and I recommended to in order

00:52:44.340 --> 00:52:47.370
to really get the transformation,

00:52:47.370 --> 00:52:52.110
going to accumulate 30 minutes
a day in the squat position,

00:52:52.110 --> 00:52:53.760
unloaded, just resting down,

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:56.700
not correct, not erect, many
people make this mistake,

00:52:56.700 --> 00:52:58.740
they didn't read through the whole thing.

00:52:58.740 --> 00:53:00.450
It's just resting down there.

00:53:00.450 --> 00:53:03.270
And of course, you have
to be mindful of dosages.

00:53:03.270 --> 00:53:06.690
Some people will get hurt if
they try to do it too quickly

00:53:06.690 --> 00:53:09.060
so they might need a
buildup process towards it.

00:53:09.060 --> 00:53:11.763
And also, I'm not talking
about 30 minutes straight,

00:53:13.110 --> 00:53:15.390
but accumulation throughout the day.

00:53:15.390 --> 00:53:18.690
And this does a lot of good
for digestive problems,

00:53:18.690 --> 00:53:22.590
for lower back pain, for
hip pains, for knees,

00:53:22.590 --> 00:53:24.690
and generally for aging

00:53:24.690 --> 00:53:28.740
because it's basically folding your body

00:53:28.740 --> 00:53:30.000
in the most basic way.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:31.050
Are you folding your body?

00:53:31.050 --> 00:53:32.820
If you're not folding your body,

00:53:32.820 --> 00:53:35.130
you will lose the
foldability of your body.

00:53:35.130 --> 00:53:38.070
And this is probably the easiest

00:53:38.070 --> 00:53:40.220
and the most abundant
way to fold the body.

00:53:41.340 --> 00:53:44.700
So, but this is an example of something

00:53:44.700 --> 00:53:47.490
that can be very useful
with many, many people,

00:53:47.490 --> 00:53:50.640
but there will always
be unique individuals

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:52.620
which needs something else.

00:53:52.620 --> 00:53:56.850
And there are benefits

00:53:56.850 --> 00:53:58.890
in examining things

00:53:58.890 --> 00:54:02.340
and also there are
benefits in getting hurt,

00:54:02.340 --> 00:54:05.970
which is not often discussed,
especially not in these parts.

00:54:05.970 --> 00:54:10.530
So, I'm one of the only ones as a teacher

00:54:10.530 --> 00:54:12.897
that says, "I injured
many of my students."

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:15.660
And if I did not do that,

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:19.980
I would be totally
useless for them as well.

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:23.793
That totally safe system
has nothing to offer,

00:54:24.630 --> 00:54:26.670
practically nothing is totally safe.

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:28.860
And we can, of course,
we don't approach it

00:54:28.860 --> 00:54:31.320
with a ballsy or machoistic thing,

00:54:31.320 --> 00:54:33.120
but we are aware that sometimes

00:54:33.120 --> 00:54:34.740
we have to go beyond the boundaries

00:54:34.740 --> 00:54:37.440
and hopefully those would
be the small injuries

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:40.050
that will help us avoid the big injuries.

00:54:40.050 --> 00:54:41.850
But if you try to avoid
the small injuries,

00:54:41.850 --> 00:54:44.730
maybe you'll get those
big injuries in there.

00:54:44.730 --> 00:54:48.690
So, examining which types
and forms of movement,

00:54:48.690 --> 00:54:53.190
the location of the
body, speed of execution,

00:54:53.190 --> 00:54:55.650
the type of organization of the body,

00:54:55.650 --> 00:54:59.550
which is a whole thing
that we can discuss.

00:54:59.550 --> 00:55:01.650
All of this is up for the grabs

00:55:01.650 --> 00:55:03.510
and something that we have to create

00:55:03.510 --> 00:55:06.420
individual relationship with,

00:55:06.420 --> 00:55:08.250
hopefully with good guidance,

00:55:08.250 --> 00:55:12.270
where we can get the right scenarios,

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:15.120
a facilitator of good
scenarios for our learning,

00:55:15.120 --> 00:55:16.830
which is what I try to do.

00:55:16.830 --> 00:55:18.690
And less of a technical state of mind,

00:55:18.690 --> 00:55:22.740
do this ABC or, yeah, like chunking

00:55:22.740 --> 00:55:25.885
what I really dislike from a long time

00:55:25.885 --> 00:55:28.747
is like many people they tell me,

00:55:28.747 --> 00:55:30.270
"Have you met this guy?

00:55:30.270 --> 00:55:31.530
He's an amazing teacher

00:55:31.530 --> 00:55:34.140
because he chunked the
process into these bits

00:55:34.140 --> 00:55:36.099
and not even in the
correct places to chunk,"

00:55:36.099 --> 00:55:38.640
it was like and it doesn't offer,

00:55:38.640 --> 00:55:40.950
it locks us, this state of mind.

00:55:40.950 --> 00:55:43.050
I talk about the chemistry model,

00:55:43.050 --> 00:55:46.470
I call it my chemistry
model where an atom,

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:49.380
a molecule, and then a
compound is conceptualized

00:55:49.380 --> 00:55:50.970
versus just chunking.

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:53.130
So, there is an actual evolution

00:55:53.130 --> 00:55:56.580
like I call it also sketch learning.

00:55:56.580 --> 00:55:58.740
I'm not going to try to draw you,

00:55:58.740 --> 00:56:01.320
if I know anything about art and drawing,

00:56:01.320 --> 00:56:04.320
I'm going to start by
capturing something very rough.

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:08.010
And I need to practice that
first, that dynamic entity,

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:11.673
before I go into the rendering
and the shading, et cetera.

00:56:13.080 --> 00:56:15.330
So, the same way to learn things,

00:56:15.330 --> 00:56:18.630
so big picture, the small details.

00:56:18.630 --> 00:56:22.320
And unlike many of my
teachers in that I ran into,

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:25.140
and I say, with the greatest respect

00:56:25.140 --> 00:56:26.760
because I don't know who taught me more,

00:56:26.760 --> 00:56:29.073
my good teachers or my worst teachers,

00:56:30.150 --> 00:56:32.910
but some of them just teach
from the small details

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:35.670
into a big picture that never arrives.

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:39.450
- Given that humans can
generate such a broad array

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:40.470
of types of movement,

00:56:40.470 --> 00:56:43.050
run, jump, duck, squat, leap,

00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:45.750
and all these types of movements.

00:56:45.750 --> 00:56:48.060
Do you think there's value
in observing the movements

00:56:48.060 --> 00:56:49.320
of other animal species?

00:56:49.320 --> 00:56:53.223
I know I certainly enjoy
watching other animals move.

00:56:54.870 --> 00:56:55.740
I think the most,

00:56:55.740 --> 00:56:59.190
one of the more spectacular animal facts

00:56:59.190 --> 00:57:02.100
that was shared with me is
when I was a graduate student,

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:05.280
someone down the hall was
working on the little pedals

00:57:05.280 --> 00:57:09.360
of the chameleon, which can walk up walls.

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:10.770
And it was a great mystery

00:57:10.770 --> 00:57:12.450
as to whether or not they were suction,

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:13.890
but turns out they can do it in a vacuum,

00:57:13.890 --> 00:57:15.060
so it's not suction.

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:16.650
Whether or not there was
some sticky substance

00:57:16.650 --> 00:57:18.690
and it turned out, I don't know,

00:57:18.690 --> 00:57:20.040
I feel compelled to share this with you

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:21.870
so I'm going to do it
because I have a feeling

00:57:21.870 --> 00:57:24.480
it will lead us to an
insight of some sort,

00:57:24.480 --> 00:57:27.330
that those little tiny pedals are so thin

00:57:27.330 --> 00:57:30.810
and so close together that the chameleon

00:57:30.810 --> 00:57:32.070
actually sticks to the wall

00:57:32.070 --> 00:57:34.470
by what are called van der Waals forces,

00:57:34.470 --> 00:57:36.570
meaning it's a very weak molecular force,

00:57:36.570 --> 00:57:38.610
but strong enough to stick to the wall

00:57:38.610 --> 00:57:40.890
because they are actually
exchanging molecules

00:57:40.890 --> 00:57:42.420
with the surface they're on.

00:57:42.420 --> 00:57:44.310
- Wow.
- So, obviously

00:57:44.310 --> 00:57:48.780
we can't do that, and yet I spent hours,

00:57:48.780 --> 00:57:50.280
because they were in the lab next door,

00:57:50.280 --> 00:57:53.130
watching videos of these
little chameleon walk

00:57:53.130 --> 00:57:56.730
and the articulation of
these feet is incredible

00:57:56.730 --> 00:57:59.700
because they're literally
rolling those little pedals along

00:57:59.700 --> 00:58:02.070
in a way that it kind of defies

00:58:02.070 --> 00:58:04.380
anything else I've ever seen.

00:58:04.380 --> 00:58:06.630
I told myself this was useful,

00:58:06.630 --> 00:58:07.950
A, because I thought it was interesting,

00:58:07.950 --> 00:58:10.530
but, B, because I never
really thought about

00:58:10.530 --> 00:58:12.090
how I articulate my foot.

00:58:12.090 --> 00:58:13.530
I've thought about being a heel striker

00:58:13.530 --> 00:58:14.910
or a toe striker when I run

00:58:14.910 --> 00:58:17.157
and no one can tell me which
one I'm supposed to be.

00:58:17.157 --> 00:58:18.720
And maybe you can tell me,
[Ido laughing]

00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:23.720
but the point is, or I
suppose the question is,

00:58:23.730 --> 00:58:25.470
do you think there's value in observing

00:58:25.470 --> 00:58:28.530
the extremes of animal kingdom movement

00:58:28.530 --> 00:58:30.630
as a way to inform the play space

00:58:30.630 --> 00:58:31.920
and the exploration space

00:58:31.920 --> 00:58:34.323
of our own human movement practice?

00:58:35.700 --> 00:58:36.533
- I think so.

00:58:36.533 --> 00:58:39.333
I think, first, it's inspiring,

00:58:41.580 --> 00:58:44.190
it opens up, but I will take it away

00:58:44.190 --> 00:58:47.370
from the romantic point of view.

00:58:47.370 --> 00:58:48.870
And I would offer another way

00:58:48.870 --> 00:58:53.020
to examine all these movements exist in us

00:58:54.120 --> 00:58:56.250
in ways, in certain ways.

00:58:56.250 --> 00:58:59.407
Like the work of Gracovetsky on the spine,

00:58:59.407 --> 00:59:01.260
"The Spinal Engine",

00:59:01.260 --> 00:59:06.260
and to see how these old ways

00:59:06.480 --> 00:59:09.240
of moving even all the
way up to exoskeletons

00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:13.680
and like primary, very ancient

00:59:13.680 --> 00:59:17.040
or even single cell
things are still within us

00:59:17.040 --> 00:59:17.873
to a certain extent.

00:59:17.873 --> 00:59:20.500
And then, of course, this gets developed

00:59:22.410 --> 00:59:25.620
like the Darwinian state of mind

00:59:25.620 --> 00:59:29.223
got stuck for many years on
the survival of the fetus.

00:59:30.360 --> 00:59:32.220
But actually, I believe,

00:59:32.220 --> 00:59:35.460
I always believed and
I saw some information

00:59:35.460 --> 00:59:39.390
about it lately that mutation
is the heart of the model,

00:59:39.390 --> 00:59:41.190
not survival of the fittest.

00:59:41.190 --> 00:59:43.260
- Yeah, people often
hear the word mutation

00:59:43.260 --> 00:59:45.330
and they think, "Oh, mutations are bad."

00:59:45.330 --> 00:59:47.940
There are maladaptive mutations

00:59:47.940 --> 00:59:50.670
and then there are adaptive
mutations, for sure.

00:59:50.670 --> 00:59:54.510
- And then, this places the
word change in the heart of it,

00:59:54.510 --> 00:59:58.200
what it wants to do, change.

00:59:58.200 --> 01:00:00.963
So, it does not want to become better,

01:00:02.520 --> 01:00:07.020
there is an inherent change in it.

01:00:07.020 --> 01:00:10.170
And then, of course, they
become better at X, Y, Z,

01:00:10.170 --> 01:00:13.770
fittest is the secondary perspective

01:00:13.770 --> 01:00:15.810
that arrives in relation
to certain things,

01:00:15.810 --> 01:00:17.280
but there is still a stronger,

01:00:17.280 --> 01:00:19.983
more ancient driving
force into the process.

01:00:20.850 --> 01:00:24.480
So, for me, this is cool
to see these animals

01:00:24.480 --> 01:00:26.730
take it all the way to this extreme,

01:00:26.730 --> 01:00:29.670
but it's also still reflecting within us.

01:00:29.670 --> 01:00:31.560
So, I love to do, like for example,

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:34.500
I introduce with people spinal waves,

01:00:34.500 --> 01:00:37.530
and by bringing these waves into the body,

01:00:37.530 --> 01:00:42.450
sometimes you get weird
experiences like emotional releases

01:00:42.450 --> 01:00:45.300
and sometimes, and other times

01:00:45.300 --> 01:00:48.780
it can become an incredible
tool to help an athlete

01:00:48.780 --> 01:00:51.810
which specialized and
reach the top of the top.

01:00:51.810 --> 01:00:54.990
And then, you defrag
his system a little bit

01:00:54.990 --> 01:00:59.400
and offer him some freshness
and some segmental movement.

01:00:59.400 --> 01:01:01.710
And first you fuck him up,

01:01:01.710 --> 01:01:03.510
that's usually the case.

01:01:03.510 --> 01:01:05.970
Technically he's off,
his coordination is off,

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:08.880
but later the growth will arrive.

01:01:08.880 --> 01:01:10.650
It's a form of playfulness,

01:01:10.650 --> 01:01:12.960
it's a form of examining things

01:01:12.960 --> 01:01:15.390
regardless of their success or failure.

01:01:15.390 --> 01:01:18.420
More understanding that
change is important

01:01:18.420 --> 01:01:19.740
and then after that,

01:01:19.740 --> 01:01:24.390
we can also look at the more
competitive state of mind

01:01:24.390 --> 01:01:27.393
and the more success
and failure orientation,

01:01:28.380 --> 01:01:30.810
but there is no game without change.

01:01:30.810 --> 01:01:33.586
So, this is the primary one,

01:01:33.586 --> 01:01:35.160
and that's why I say, "Okay,

01:01:35.160 --> 01:01:38.130
you want to succeed in the
tasks like we did earlier,

01:01:38.130 --> 01:01:40.680
but you stayed within the
game, to sustain the game,

01:01:40.680 --> 01:01:44.970
the infinite versus
finite game perspective."

01:01:44.970 --> 01:01:49.200
To sustain the game means
to continue to change,

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:50.793
continue to transform,

01:01:51.780 --> 01:01:55.293
and then to win the game
sometimes mean game over.

01:01:57.210 --> 01:01:59.910
So, it's like, yeah, within that tension

01:01:59.910 --> 01:02:04.530
I think it's beautiful to
play, and to exist, and to be.

01:02:04.530 --> 01:02:06.150
- You mentioned something that for me

01:02:06.150 --> 01:02:08.760
is an incredibly important concept

01:02:08.760 --> 01:02:09.690
for a couple of reasons,

01:02:09.690 --> 01:02:11.640
and you mentioned these spinal waves.

01:02:11.640 --> 01:02:15.030
Right, I have to assume that's
taking the torso for us,

01:02:15.030 --> 01:02:17.220
you know, movement morons

01:02:17.220 --> 01:02:21.000
that I'll just refer to in
coarse terms of thoracic spine.

01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:23.400
So, I mean, I will stay away
from the technical anatomy

01:02:23.400 --> 01:02:25.830
and the torso and creating movement

01:02:25.830 --> 01:02:28.950
either side to side undulation

01:02:28.950 --> 01:02:32.220
or arching and extension of the spine.

01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:34.650
- Yeah, dorsal, ventral, side to side,

01:02:34.650 --> 01:02:36.663
or rotational as well as spiraling.

01:02:39.960 --> 01:02:43.170
- Have you ever had the
experience that of yourself

01:02:43.170 --> 01:02:45.330
or other people engaging
in those types of movements

01:02:45.330 --> 01:02:48.750
and experiencing particular
categories of emotions?

01:02:48.750 --> 01:02:50.460
And I have a particular
reason for asking this,

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:51.930
there's no right or
wrong answer, of course,

01:02:51.930 --> 01:02:54.570
but I'm just curious
whether or not movement

01:02:54.570 --> 01:02:56.640
of let's call it the core of the body,

01:02:56.640 --> 01:02:57.960
things close to the midline

01:02:57.960 --> 01:03:01.230
as opposed to far away from the
midline like the digits far.

01:03:01.230 --> 01:03:02.672
Is there any,

01:03:02.672 --> 01:03:04.500
do you have any evidence
that that can evoke

01:03:04.500 --> 01:03:07.950
a certain category of emotional states?

01:03:07.950 --> 01:03:09.300
- Evidence, I have none,

01:03:09.300 --> 01:03:12.783
but I have experience and I
have some thoughts about it.

01:03:14.400 --> 01:03:18.990
Ida Rolf is known to have created Rolfing

01:03:18.990 --> 01:03:20.220
or structural integration

01:03:20.220 --> 01:03:23.070
said, "The issues are in the tissues."

01:03:23.070 --> 01:03:27.180
And around the spine, the spine is us,

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:30.120
as you know, it's like can
take an arm off, a limb,

01:03:30.120 --> 01:03:33.660
but there's been attempts,

01:03:33.660 --> 01:03:36.720
but there is no brainy alone,

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:38.610
this cerebral thing alone,

01:03:38.610 --> 01:03:42.240
the spine and the maybe
more parts of n-systems

01:03:42.240 --> 01:03:44.010
inside the torso are important.

01:03:44.010 --> 01:03:46.800
So, that's why I like to
start from that core entity.

01:03:46.800 --> 01:03:50.763
And then these little
fluctuations they create,

01:03:53.400 --> 01:03:56.820
they unblock things, they
start to move things.

01:03:56.820 --> 01:03:59.730
And you can avoid, funny enough,

01:03:59.730 --> 01:04:04.620
mobilizing those areas by
doing big frame motions,

01:04:04.620 --> 01:04:07.533
and competitive motions, and
techniques all your life.

01:04:08.490 --> 01:04:11.220
So, even most yogis, for example,

01:04:11.220 --> 01:04:12.663
they look extremely mobile,

01:04:13.560 --> 01:04:15.900
but then when you're actually
going into the small,

01:04:15.900 --> 01:04:17.640
what I call the small frame,

01:04:17.640 --> 01:04:20.190
I borrowed this from Chinese martial arts,

01:04:20.190 --> 01:04:21.570
small frame, big frame.

01:04:21.570 --> 01:04:24.630
The big frame is this big changes

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:28.260
of our total body in space posture.

01:04:28.260 --> 01:04:30.390
And then the small frame is barely moving,

01:04:30.390 --> 01:04:33.330
but mobilizing the
little bits that comprise

01:04:33.330 --> 01:04:35.790
the same pretty much posture.

01:04:35.790 --> 01:04:37.230
So, these are very beneficial

01:04:37.230 --> 01:04:40.893
and it has totally disappeared
from our physical culture.

01:04:41.730 --> 01:04:43.263
When you introduce it back,

01:04:44.790 --> 01:04:47.190
the small frame offers the big frame,

01:04:47.190 --> 01:04:49.410
but the big frame doesn't
offer the small frame

01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:52.200
because, of course, the
small detail come together

01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:53.430
into the big picture.

01:04:53.430 --> 01:04:56.130
So, if I want to place my
body in a specific position

01:04:56.130 --> 01:04:58.170
and I have all these bits moving well,

01:04:58.170 --> 01:05:00.840
I can construct it in whatever way I want.

01:05:00.840 --> 01:05:02.703
But if I just work on the big one,

01:05:03.900 --> 01:05:07.440
most chances are I just
mobilize certain areas

01:05:07.440 --> 01:05:11.070
while other areas are
totally held or blocked

01:05:11.070 --> 01:05:14.580
and then I'm specialized one more time.

01:05:14.580 --> 01:05:17.910
Take me out of this realm
and I'll have difficulties.

01:05:17.910 --> 01:05:20.340
What will sit there in this stagnation?

01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:25.140
Emotion, material, thoughts, traumas.

01:05:25.140 --> 01:05:27.453
That's why people get discharges.

01:05:29.340 --> 01:05:32.580
The body memory is not
what we think it is,

01:05:32.580 --> 01:05:33.413
that's how I believe,

01:05:33.413 --> 01:05:36.813
it is stored in a lot everywhere,

01:05:38.340 --> 01:05:40.680
and I've had those experiences.

01:05:40.680 --> 01:05:42.690
A lot of people have the opposite.

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:45.090
When a certain emotion is evoked,

01:05:45.090 --> 01:05:47.103
they start to undulate the spine.

01:05:48.120 --> 01:05:50.370
So, this can be worked from this direction

01:05:50.370 --> 01:05:51.330
or from this direction.

01:05:51.330 --> 01:05:55.473
And I believe by applying such a practice,

01:05:57.280 --> 01:06:01.590
it is wise, you basically
turn over the lens

01:06:01.590 --> 01:06:04.330
and you are allowing things

01:06:05.913 --> 01:06:08.400
to shift, and to move, and to adapt.

01:06:08.400 --> 01:06:09.990
So, I highly recommend it

01:06:09.990 --> 01:06:13.615
and we teach it in a very
elaborate and gradual way.

01:06:13.615 --> 01:06:17.160
And this is needed really

01:06:17.160 --> 01:06:19.020
because people, when they just go

01:06:19.020 --> 01:06:20.910
into like some general recommendation,

01:06:20.910 --> 01:06:23.287
they usually just get
stuck into a new pattern,

01:06:23.287 --> 01:06:24.540
"Ah, that spinal wave,

01:06:24.540 --> 01:06:25.890
okay, that's it."

01:06:25.890 --> 01:06:26.910
So, I've been using,

01:06:26.910 --> 01:06:28.950
again, this slice and dice-like teaching

01:06:28.950 --> 01:06:31.740
dozens of systems of moving the torso

01:06:31.740 --> 01:06:35.070
until a person is freed
to really move the torso

01:06:35.070 --> 01:06:36.930
like the language is created,

01:06:36.930 --> 01:06:40.770
the small enough units are
created in your understanding

01:06:40.770 --> 01:06:42.450
from all these systems

01:06:42.450 --> 01:06:43.650
and then you improvise,

01:06:43.650 --> 01:06:45.850
you reach the highest
level of the practice.

01:06:46.800 --> 01:06:48.480
- I love the answer.

01:06:48.480 --> 01:06:51.363
Let me tell you a bit of why I asked.

01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:56.490
So, there's a principle in neuroscience,

01:06:56.490 --> 01:06:58.710
but especially in neuroevolution,

01:06:58.710 --> 01:07:01.590
they call it evo-devo sometimes,
evolution and development,

01:07:01.590 --> 01:07:02.580
how those link.

01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:04.590
If you look at, so we have motor neurons,

01:07:04.590 --> 01:07:06.090
as you know, but for the audience,

01:07:06.090 --> 01:07:07.260
that live in our spinal cord

01:07:07.260 --> 01:07:09.720
that cause transmission and
contraction of the muscles,

01:07:09.720 --> 01:07:11.460
allows us to move our limbs.

01:07:11.460 --> 01:07:13.080
And then, we have motor neurons up here

01:07:13.080 --> 01:07:13.980
called upper motor neurons

01:07:13.980 --> 01:07:15.750
that control the motor lower ones.

01:07:15.750 --> 01:07:18.390
So, once something is
reflexive or learned,

01:07:18.390 --> 01:07:19.740
we're not thinking about it, so to speak,

01:07:19.740 --> 01:07:21.420
we mainly use the lower motor neurons.

01:07:21.420 --> 01:07:23.220
We know this because you
can do an experiment,

01:07:23.220 --> 01:07:24.630
it's a rather barbaric experiment,

01:07:24.630 --> 01:07:26.640
but it's been done many times

01:07:26.640 --> 01:07:28.920
called creating a decerebrate cat.

01:07:28.920 --> 01:07:31.050
You actually remove the neocortex

01:07:31.050 --> 01:07:33.030
and these cats will walk on a treadmill,

01:07:33.030 --> 01:07:35.550
it's called fictive
motion, no problem at all.

01:07:35.550 --> 01:07:38.430
There are human beings
who don't have a neocortex

01:07:38.430 --> 01:07:40.410
or much of their neocortex is missing,

01:07:40.410 --> 01:07:42.357
they generate perfectly fine movement.

01:07:42.357 --> 01:07:44.280
- The pattern has been download.

01:07:44.280 --> 01:07:47.880
- That's right, and it's truly
downloaded into the spine

01:07:47.880 --> 01:07:49.980
and the connection between
the spine and muscles.

01:07:49.980 --> 01:07:51.360
Now, the motor neurons

01:07:51.360 --> 01:07:54.693
that control the spinal
waves, as you called them,

01:07:55.650 --> 01:07:58.290
are of a particular category.

01:07:58.290 --> 01:08:01.230
They have a molecular signature,
a physiological signature,

01:08:01.230 --> 01:08:03.420
they were identified by, he's dead now,

01:08:03.420 --> 01:08:07.140
but a biologist at Columbia
University named Tom Jessell,

01:08:07.140 --> 01:08:09.000
and many of his scientific offspring.

01:08:09.000 --> 01:08:10.710
Here's what's interesting,

01:08:10.710 --> 01:08:14.970
in fish or in animals that
really only have the opportunity

01:08:14.970 --> 01:08:19.023
to undulate and flap their little fins.

01:08:20.580 --> 01:08:21.780
Though, motor neurons

01:08:21.780 --> 01:08:24.600
that control undulation in those animals

01:08:24.600 --> 01:08:28.140
are identical molecularly to
the motor neurons that control

01:08:28.140 --> 01:08:30.600
the spinal undulation in humans.

01:08:30.600 --> 01:08:34.560
What's been added in human
evolution are extra rows,

01:08:34.560 --> 01:08:37.350
literally categories, of
molecularly distinct neurons

01:08:37.350 --> 01:08:40.680
so that as you move from the
center of the body outward,

01:08:40.680 --> 01:08:43.470
unlike a fish, which can move its fins,

01:08:43.470 --> 01:08:45.870
but can't actually, it
doesn't have digits.

01:08:45.870 --> 01:08:49.020
We have special motor neurons
to move these little bits,

01:08:49.020 --> 01:08:51.240
these bits, these bits,

01:08:51.240 --> 01:08:53.040
and I can't do a spinal wave,

01:08:53.040 --> 01:08:55.440
but I can do the mudras thing,

01:08:55.440 --> 01:08:56.807
like the belly thing.

01:08:56.807 --> 01:08:58.320
- Ah-ha.
- That comes from seeing

01:08:58.320 --> 01:08:59.850
the movie "E.T." when I was a kid

01:08:59.850 --> 01:09:01.110
and puffing out my stomach
[Ido laughing]

01:09:01.110 --> 01:09:03.180
and then realizing that I could wave it,

01:09:03.180 --> 01:09:04.680
but only in one direction and-

01:09:04.680 --> 01:09:05.820
Okay.
- apparently not up.

01:09:05.820 --> 01:09:10.560
Anyway, the yogis out there
can chuckle at that, but.

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:12.450
- The yogis actually do it to the side.

01:09:12.450 --> 01:09:13.560
- Oh, do they?
- Yeah.

01:09:13.560 --> 01:09:15.540
- I don't know if I can do that.

01:09:15.540 --> 01:09:19.530
Anyway, my spinal wave is
weak, but I'll work on it.

01:09:19.530 --> 01:09:22.860
But what I find so
interesting about these layers

01:09:22.860 --> 01:09:24.960
of, I don't want to say sophistication,

01:09:24.960 --> 01:09:27.960
but these with evolution

01:09:27.960 --> 01:09:30.930
came the addition of more
pools of opportunity.

01:09:30.930 --> 01:09:32.430
These motor neuron
pools, as they're called,

01:09:32.430 --> 01:09:35.160
are opportunity to engage in new,

01:09:35.160 --> 01:09:36.990
more elaborate types of movement.

01:09:36.990 --> 01:09:39.750
But with each new pool
became the opportunity

01:09:39.750 --> 01:09:42.750
to create combinations of new movement.

01:09:42.750 --> 01:09:45.300
And so, the reason I
asked you why spinal waves

01:09:45.300 --> 01:09:46.980
create one category of movement

01:09:46.980 --> 01:09:50.220
is that if you touch a fish
on one side of its body,

01:09:50.220 --> 01:09:51.870
it moves to the opposite side,

01:09:51.870 --> 01:09:53.550
it never moves toward it.

01:09:53.550 --> 01:09:55.020
But earlier we were doing a practice,

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:58.200
somewhat similar of testing
this similar reflex.

01:09:58.200 --> 01:10:02.910
And sometimes I or someone
will move toward a touch,

01:10:02.910 --> 01:10:05.520
we don't deviate to the opposite side.

01:10:05.520 --> 01:10:10.520
So, I have this untested,

01:10:10.530 --> 01:10:12.300
at least formally tested hypothesis

01:10:12.300 --> 01:10:15.030
that movements of small digits

01:10:15.030 --> 01:10:17.010
and portions of our
distal, as they're called,

01:10:17.010 --> 01:10:19.680
far from the midline body parts

01:10:19.680 --> 01:10:21.600
evoke different sensations,

01:10:21.600 --> 01:10:23.790
maybe even far more subtle sensations

01:10:23.790 --> 01:10:25.644
than movements of the core of our body

01:10:25.644 --> 01:10:29.160
and the stuff closer to the spine.

01:10:29.160 --> 01:10:30.570
Again, it's just a theory,

01:10:30.570 --> 01:10:33.330
but I'm grateful for your answer

01:10:33.330 --> 01:10:36.150
because it lands at least in
the general vector direction

01:10:36.150 --> 01:10:39.540
of my idea here.

01:10:39.540 --> 01:10:44.540
- The central orientation is
mostly gone from our culture,

01:10:44.610 --> 01:10:47.940
we don't even walk basically these days.

01:10:47.940 --> 01:10:49.800
If you look at traditional culture,

01:10:49.800 --> 01:10:54.030
the amount of walking you
do on a rest day, it's huge.

01:10:54.030 --> 01:10:57.660
And so, we started to create technologies

01:10:57.660 --> 01:11:00.540
to bring everything into the periphery,

01:11:00.540 --> 01:11:02.700
controlling it with the
fingertips, et cetera.

01:11:02.700 --> 01:11:05.910
So, we have incredible
neurological development

01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:09.870
relating to this, but
our central patterns,

01:11:09.870 --> 01:11:13.740
swimming, running, jumping, throwing,

01:11:13.740 --> 01:11:15.930
throwing is not pushing away,

01:11:15.930 --> 01:11:17.010
that's an example, right?

01:11:17.010 --> 01:11:19.053
- Like some people when you
give them a ball to throw,

01:11:19.053 --> 01:11:20.730
that you can tell if they've never

01:11:20.730 --> 01:11:21.563
thrown a ball before.
- Yeah,

01:11:21.563 --> 01:11:22.938
they throw like a girl.
- Yeah, they don't just-

01:11:22.938 --> 01:11:24.571
- That is often said
[Andrew laughing]

01:11:24.571 --> 01:11:25.587
here in the US.

01:11:25.587 --> 01:11:26.790
And it's, of course, unfair,

01:11:26.790 --> 01:11:29.520
but it relates to experience, right?

01:11:29.520 --> 01:11:32.970
That is less maybe promoted

01:11:32.970 --> 01:11:34.410
or offered for females,

01:11:34.410 --> 01:11:37.890
so you get this peripheral pattern

01:11:37.890 --> 01:11:40.440
instead of a central-generated pattern

01:11:40.440 --> 01:11:43.173
that progresses towards the extremities.

01:11:44.580 --> 01:11:47.490
One thing I wanted to ask you

01:11:47.490 --> 01:11:51.540
is I know an area that
is not often mentioned

01:11:51.540 --> 01:11:54.520
is that some of these
ancient patterns and systems

01:11:55.680 --> 01:11:57.810
are primary in many ways,

01:11:57.810 --> 01:12:01.500
hence those newer
developments inside of us

01:12:01.500 --> 01:12:04.710
are constrained by using the connections

01:12:04.710 --> 01:12:07.050
running through these ancient systems.

01:12:07.050 --> 01:12:11.910
Hence, we are much more limited

01:12:11.910 --> 01:12:15.510
by the gene pool.

01:12:15.510 --> 01:12:19.920
We are hitchhikers on a
piece of DNA, I like to say,

01:12:19.920 --> 01:12:23.970
and that gene pool is like is
driving something so primary

01:12:23.970 --> 01:12:28.770
that even when you are in
kind of the driver's seat

01:12:28.770 --> 01:12:30.900
in your eyes, you're actually not

01:12:30.900 --> 01:12:34.383
or you're being totally
constrained by that.

01:12:35.760 --> 01:12:37.830
And I wanted to hear about this.

01:12:37.830 --> 01:12:41.520
- Yeah, recently we had
a guest on the podcast

01:12:41.520 --> 01:12:44.370
named Erich Jarvis, he's a
professor at Rockefeller,

01:12:44.370 --> 01:12:47.400
who was offered a position to dance

01:12:47.400 --> 01:12:49.080
with the Alvin Ailey dance company.

01:12:49.080 --> 01:12:50.550
So, an accomplished dancer

01:12:50.550 --> 01:12:51.840
and comes from a musical family

01:12:51.840 --> 01:12:53.940
chose to become a
neuroscientist instead and study

01:12:53.940 --> 01:12:55.140
speech and language.
- Wow.

01:12:55.140 --> 01:12:56.610
But he said something incredible,

01:12:56.610 --> 01:12:57.957
several incredible things,

01:12:57.957 --> 01:13:00.857
and really looking forward to
getting your reflections on.

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:05.850
First of all, he said that
when you look at the species

01:13:05.850 --> 01:13:09.150
in the kingdom of animals, including us,

01:13:09.150 --> 01:13:12.903
that have elaborate
language and true song,

01:13:14.190 --> 01:13:18.090
they all also have the capacity to dance.

01:13:18.090 --> 01:13:18.923
All the...

01:13:18.923 --> 01:13:19.890
It turns out hummingbirds

01:13:19.890 --> 01:13:22.860
actually have a dance and a song capacity.

01:13:22.860 --> 01:13:26.310
That perhaps, and this is the going idea

01:13:26.310 --> 01:13:29.640
now in neuroscience and
evolution of the brain,

01:13:29.640 --> 01:13:32.710
that singing actually came before

01:13:33.720 --> 01:13:36.360
finally articulated speech and language,

01:13:36.360 --> 01:13:39.570
that voice involved first
to sing, to communicate.

01:13:39.570 --> 01:13:42.900
I mean, to enunciate ugh, or uh, or mm.

01:13:42.900 --> 01:13:46.260
You know, but then song
may have come first.

01:13:46.260 --> 01:13:49.050
Where you have song, you have dance

01:13:49.050 --> 01:13:50.160
and the capacity to dance,

01:13:50.160 --> 01:13:52.080
which of course is movement of the body.

01:13:52.080 --> 01:13:54.330
And where you have song and dance,

01:13:54.330 --> 01:13:56.700
you always find that those species

01:13:56.700 --> 01:13:58.950
can generate elaborate language.

01:13:58.950 --> 01:14:01.530
Now, the simple version of this is okay,

01:14:01.530 --> 01:14:03.840
sophisticated brains
tend to create clusters

01:14:03.840 --> 01:14:05.910
of sophisticated capabilities,

01:14:05.910 --> 01:14:07.110
but the other possibility,

01:14:07.110 --> 01:14:08.730
and it's the one that Jarvis proposes

01:14:08.730 --> 01:14:09.690
and I think it's in line

01:14:09.690 --> 01:14:11.580
with what you're perhaps raising here

01:14:11.580 --> 01:14:14.460
is the idea that movement of the body

01:14:14.460 --> 01:14:17.790
and range and sophistication
of movement of the body

01:14:17.790 --> 01:14:20.100
through all these different systems

01:14:20.100 --> 01:14:24.210
may have actually promoted
or even driven the evolution

01:14:24.210 --> 01:14:29.100
of the things that we think
of as speech and language

01:14:29.100 --> 01:14:33.240
and the ability to have multiple
words for the same concept,

01:14:33.240 --> 01:14:36.270
or to have elaborate
articulation of speech.

01:14:36.270 --> 01:14:39.540
I find this incredibly
attractive as an idea

01:14:39.540 --> 01:14:43.260
because certainly from
as a hierarchy of needs,

01:14:43.260 --> 01:14:45.150
we needed to move first to survive,

01:14:45.150 --> 01:14:47.493
and to mate, and to flee, and to attack.

01:14:48.330 --> 01:14:49.980
It makes perfect sense
to me that the layers

01:14:49.980 --> 01:14:52.620
would be built up fundamentally
from the body to the mind

01:14:52.620 --> 01:14:55.200
and not the other way around.

01:14:55.200 --> 01:14:56.250
So, that's one piece,

01:14:56.250 --> 01:14:57.540
and then the other piece,

01:14:57.540 --> 01:15:00.690
which I'll just share for any
reflections you might have

01:15:00.690 --> 01:15:02.430
that just blew me away

01:15:02.430 --> 01:15:07.083
was Jarvis told me that when we read,

01:15:08.220 --> 01:15:10.530
if, and this has been done experimentally,

01:15:10.530 --> 01:15:12.510
if one records the EMG,

01:15:12.510 --> 01:15:16.140
the low level muscular activity
in the larynx and pharynx,

01:15:16.140 --> 01:15:19.590
we are actually repeating
the words that we read,

01:15:19.590 --> 01:15:23.700
but so subtly so that we
don't actually speak them out,

01:15:23.700 --> 01:15:26.160
unless there's some sort
of neurologic deficit,

01:15:26.160 --> 01:15:28.560
which some people have, some
people mumble while they read.

01:15:28.560 --> 01:15:33.060
But what that tells me is
that language is movement

01:15:33.060 --> 01:15:35.100
and movement is language.

01:15:35.100 --> 01:15:37.230
So, again, we have this convergence,

01:15:37.230 --> 01:15:39.240
but at a very basic level

01:15:39.240 --> 01:15:42.060
I'd love your reflections
on those are all his ideas,

01:15:42.060 --> 01:15:44.220
I want to say, I'm just
repeating what he said

01:15:44.220 --> 01:15:47.580
and not nearly as precisely as he did,

01:15:47.580 --> 01:15:51.180
but how do you think of movement

01:15:51.180 --> 01:15:56.180
as either the foundation of
language or as its own language

01:15:56.850 --> 01:15:58.830
that perhaps even defies words?

01:15:58.830 --> 01:15:59.663
- Mm, [gasps].

01:16:01.740 --> 01:16:04.890
Wow, those are beautiful perspectives,

01:16:04.890 --> 01:16:07.323
and I definitely feel the same.

01:16:08.490 --> 01:16:10.770
There's a lot to say
about singing and dancing

01:16:10.770 --> 01:16:12.750
as well as also as a form

01:16:12.750 --> 01:16:17.750
of ancient programs of transmission.

01:16:20.520 --> 01:16:22.980
Sometimes that there is this

01:16:22.980 --> 01:16:27.210
in some ancient practices, the mantras.

01:16:27.210 --> 01:16:30.510
And people don't realize

01:16:30.510 --> 01:16:33.900
that they are tantric practices,

01:16:33.900 --> 01:16:38.560
they contain a form of
vibrating and breathing

01:16:41.790 --> 01:16:45.600
all tied together into very elaborate way

01:16:45.600 --> 01:16:48.060
to promote a certain effect.

01:16:48.060 --> 01:16:50.520
And how would you do something
like this in ancient times?

01:16:50.520 --> 01:16:52.170
This is ingenious.

01:16:52.170 --> 01:16:53.550
We, even until today,

01:16:53.550 --> 01:16:55.347
we need a full book to describe something

01:16:55.347 --> 01:16:57.480
and it wouldn't work as well,

01:16:57.480 --> 01:17:00.543
so it's like a very ancient
form of transmission.

01:17:01.950 --> 01:17:05.163
The more accurate we
became with the language,

01:17:06.450 --> 01:17:08.730
the more dead it became,

01:17:08.730 --> 01:17:12.600
because it is less of a movement entity,

01:17:12.600 --> 01:17:15.930
it is less of a dynamic
entity from its nature.

01:17:15.930 --> 01:17:19.740
And that's why Yukio Mishima
says, "It's corrupting,

01:17:19.740 --> 01:17:21.057
it corrupts us."

01:17:23.340 --> 01:17:24.600
So, definitely, definitely

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:29.160
the conducing force

01:17:29.160 --> 01:17:34.160
or the primary force for me is
movement that is experienced.

01:17:34.920 --> 01:17:37.200
Every time we talk about movement,

01:17:37.200 --> 01:17:41.220
basically, even now we are
spilling it into a container

01:17:41.220 --> 01:17:44.613
to call it what it is,
but it is beyond that.

01:17:45.870 --> 01:17:49.020
So, then it is applied into dancing,

01:17:49.020 --> 01:17:51.153
into singing, into language.

01:17:52.260 --> 01:17:56.490
There is no other language
that I see as a primary mode,

01:17:56.490 --> 01:18:01.443
and this is a nature of
space, time, things moving.

01:18:03.390 --> 01:18:06.480
So, I think everything moves

01:18:06.480 --> 01:18:09.450
into the direction of
understanding that more and more,

01:18:09.450 --> 01:18:13.440
and maybe it's not so
popular to call it movement

01:18:13.440 --> 01:18:15.180
or people have some connotations,

01:18:15.180 --> 01:18:17.040
and it's okay, you can
throw away this word

01:18:17.040 --> 01:18:19.650
and put another word, and
we probably need to do that

01:18:19.650 --> 01:18:20.880
also like regularly,

01:18:20.880 --> 01:18:24.123
like I start to see the
end of this word for me.

01:18:25.500 --> 01:18:29.130
Things get corrupted
again, overused, abused,

01:18:29.130 --> 01:18:32.850
and then we need a new word.

01:18:32.850 --> 01:18:36.750
And that's even that word is
only needed for communication

01:18:36.750 --> 01:18:41.490
and for specific processes
of education exchange,

01:18:41.490 --> 01:18:44.340
it's important to stay
within the experiences.

01:18:44.340 --> 01:18:47.220
It's important to continue
to promote scenarios

01:18:47.220 --> 01:18:49.473
in which the experience is primary,

01:18:50.790 --> 01:18:52.800
more open experience, let's say,

01:18:52.800 --> 01:18:55.980
and not try to hold down

01:18:55.980 --> 01:18:59.820
and define overly accurately

01:18:59.820 --> 01:19:03.453
or if it's done throwing
it away and starting again.

01:19:04.380 --> 01:19:05.910
So, there is no winning concept,

01:19:05.910 --> 01:19:08.343
you got to the winning
concept, you got nothing.

01:19:09.840 --> 01:19:12.720
You were able to grab
it, you were able to,

01:19:12.720 --> 01:19:14.060
this is very science, right?

01:19:14.060 --> 01:19:16.590
It's like, "We got it, we got it,"

01:19:16.590 --> 01:19:19.208
and then it turns out to be nothing.

01:19:19.208 --> 01:19:23.160
And like more and more time passes

01:19:23.160 --> 01:19:26.760
I feel science is becoming more humble

01:19:26.760 --> 01:19:31.760
and things are being
discussed in this way.

01:19:32.190 --> 01:19:37.190
And because really what does science do?

01:19:37.560 --> 01:19:39.450
Report the sun came up

01:19:39.450 --> 01:19:42.870
a certain amount of billions of times

01:19:42.870 --> 01:19:46.230
and then tomorrow it will come up again.

01:19:46.230 --> 01:19:48.510
It's statistics.
- Yeah, it's good prediction.

01:19:48.510 --> 01:19:51.480
- Yeah, yeah, but we can go beyond,

01:19:51.480 --> 01:19:54.720
there is something inside
of us that can go beyond.

01:19:54.720 --> 01:19:58.020
Hard to communicate, I can't
offer it right now here,

01:19:58.020 --> 01:20:01.560
but I have the experience and
thankfully I have a practice

01:20:01.560 --> 01:20:05.760
and a way to sense it, to
feel it, and to reexamine it,

01:20:05.760 --> 01:20:09.483
and then we can talk about it,
and have something from that.

01:20:10.523 --> 01:20:13.230
- Edward Wilson, the great sociobiologist,

01:20:13.230 --> 01:20:15.030
he actually founded the
field of sociobiology,

01:20:15.030 --> 01:20:17.730
E. O. Wilson, they call
him, Edward Wilson,

01:20:17.730 --> 01:20:20.340
had this beautiful word
and indeed named a book.

01:20:20.340 --> 01:20:22.530
Actually, the word was better
than the book, sorry Wilson,

01:20:22.530 --> 01:20:25.620
but the book was a little
bit meandering for my taste,

01:20:25.620 --> 01:20:29.100
but then, again, he's the
Harvard professor, not me.

01:20:29.100 --> 01:20:31.023
Well, Stanford's pretty darn good.

01:20:32.280 --> 01:20:33.720
This word is consilience,

01:20:33.720 --> 01:20:35.340
this idea of a leaping together

01:20:35.340 --> 01:20:37.080
of divergent forms of knowledge

01:20:37.080 --> 01:20:41.010
to create a truly valuable
concept, which I love.

01:20:41.010 --> 01:20:43.260
I love it because, of
course, I'm formally trained

01:20:43.260 --> 01:20:44.490
as a scientist, I look at things

01:20:44.490 --> 01:20:45.900
mainly through the lens of neuroscience,

01:20:45.900 --> 01:20:50.900
but experience is real
and observation is real.

01:20:51.510 --> 01:20:52.980
And even in the field of medicine,

01:20:52.980 --> 01:20:56.310
you have double-blind
placebo-controlled clinical trials

01:20:56.310 --> 01:21:01.140
and then you have case
studies, n-of-1, right?

01:21:01.140 --> 01:21:02.190
Not often discussed,

01:21:02.190 --> 01:21:03.270
right, I mean, H.M.,

01:21:03.270 --> 01:21:06.330
the most famous example in neuroscience

01:21:06.330 --> 01:21:08.730
of a patient that had no hippocampus

01:21:08.730 --> 01:21:11.010
informed us more about
the process of memory

01:21:11.010 --> 01:21:13.350
and indeed the function of the hippocampus

01:21:13.350 --> 01:21:17.460
than thousands of independent
experiments that followed.

01:21:17.460 --> 01:21:19.530
So, you can't have one,

01:21:19.530 --> 01:21:21.570
you need all these different
forms of exploration,

01:21:21.570 --> 01:21:25.290
which is I think we share the belief,

01:21:25.290 --> 01:21:28.590
if I may, that convergent
forms of knowledge,

01:21:28.590 --> 01:21:30.960
eventually this process of consilience

01:21:30.960 --> 01:21:33.360
can eject a new concept.

01:21:33.360 --> 01:21:34.440
And yet, the challenge,

01:21:34.440 --> 01:21:37.350
again, is that if we don't
have a language for it,

01:21:37.350 --> 01:21:38.883
it becomes hard to transmit.

01:21:39.810 --> 01:21:44.370
One of the things that I find incredibly,

01:21:44.370 --> 01:21:46.050
I'll use this word again, sticky

01:21:46.050 --> 01:21:48.630
is this notion of movement culture.

01:21:48.630 --> 01:21:50.280
I don't know who coined that phrase,

01:21:50.280 --> 01:21:52.590
or I've seen it in the circles

01:21:52.590 --> 01:21:56.220
and accounts around your
Instagram account and others.

01:21:56.220 --> 01:21:58.890
I don't know if that's a
phrase that you coined,

01:21:58.890 --> 01:22:02.010
but this idea of engaging in
movement practice with others,

01:22:02.010 --> 01:22:05.430
whether or not it's dance
or other movement practices

01:22:05.430 --> 01:22:09.090
because it's so dynamic, there's
the unpredictability of it.

01:22:09.090 --> 01:22:10.710
Even to like today,

01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:13.770
two practitioners at vastly
different levels of knowledge

01:22:13.770 --> 01:22:15.570
and experience in movement practice,

01:22:15.570 --> 01:22:17.910
there's information, I
like to think, to be gained

01:22:17.910 --> 01:22:19.710
from both sides. [laughs]

01:22:19.710 --> 01:22:21.600
So-
- 100%.

01:22:21.600 --> 01:22:25.560
So, one thing that I've
heard you say before,

01:22:25.560 --> 01:22:27.330
which really resonated with me

01:22:27.330 --> 01:22:31.140
is this idea that people have
maybe particularly in the US

01:22:31.140 --> 01:22:35.100
have this concept of, "Oh,
I have my yoga friends

01:22:35.100 --> 01:22:37.230
or the people I dance with are distinct

01:22:37.230 --> 01:22:39.780
from my family friends,
are distinct from."

01:22:39.780 --> 01:22:43.890
But as you pointed out,
gathering around movement

01:22:43.890 --> 01:22:46.980
is an age-old tradition.

01:22:46.980 --> 01:22:49.680
And that perhaps we
better off not thinking

01:22:49.680 --> 01:22:52.230
about people we exercise
with or train with,

01:22:52.230 --> 01:22:56.910
but that friendship and
connection made through movement

01:22:56.910 --> 01:23:00.750
is perhaps the most
valuable form of connection.

01:23:00.750 --> 01:23:03.570
- Yeah, I think it's a
product of those practices

01:23:03.570 --> 01:23:07.260
that are maybe not so aware

01:23:07.260 --> 01:23:10.350
or not so movement-oriented
in the open sense

01:23:10.350 --> 01:23:13.920
and then you get this
sensation with people,

01:23:13.920 --> 01:23:16.050
but alone, we do nothing,

01:23:16.050 --> 01:23:19.260
so much so that we're never alone

01:23:19.260 --> 01:23:22.590
also on the inside and we will manufacture

01:23:22.590 --> 01:23:26.973
and produce entities inside,

01:23:27.990 --> 01:23:29.100
so we're constantly

01:23:29.100 --> 01:23:31.500
in a dynamic exchange, cultural exchange.

01:23:31.500 --> 01:23:35.613
And practically, I learned
this lesson in capoeira,

01:23:37.200 --> 01:23:39.660
it's a cultural manifestation.

01:23:39.660 --> 01:23:44.010
Things happen within this context,

01:23:44.010 --> 01:23:45.990
we rub against reality,

01:23:45.990 --> 01:23:47.620
we rub against each other

01:23:48.990 --> 01:23:52.020
and their movement occurs

01:23:52.020 --> 01:23:56.867
and their insight is to be
gained and development happens

01:24:00.210 --> 01:24:03.060
and then comes other thoughts,

01:24:03.060 --> 01:24:05.913
collective knowledge
versus self-knowledge.

01:24:06.900 --> 01:24:08.850
We are transmitting knowledge.

01:24:08.850 --> 01:24:11.790
If we go on top of some mountain,

01:24:11.790 --> 01:24:16.453
20 people, 20 normal individuals,

01:24:17.862 --> 01:24:19.300
and we spent 20 years

01:24:20.580 --> 01:24:23.970
just fighting four hours in the morning,

01:24:23.970 --> 01:24:25.563
four hours in the afternoon,

01:24:26.850 --> 01:24:28.383
and we do it for 20 years,

01:24:29.250 --> 01:24:32.430
but we're isolated from any
other source of knowledge,

01:24:32.430 --> 01:24:35.010
we would still not reach anything

01:24:35.010 --> 01:24:39.307
that a very young fighter these days has.

01:24:41.100 --> 01:24:43.860
We will be unable to develop

01:24:43.860 --> 01:24:46.020
those techniques, those insights,

01:24:46.020 --> 01:24:47.730
that's where collective knowledge comes in

01:24:47.730 --> 01:24:51.240
and transmission jumps us forward,

01:24:51.240 --> 01:24:53.110
but what is the problem with that?

01:24:53.110 --> 01:24:56.730
Staying within just those
technical constraints

01:24:56.730 --> 01:24:58.290
and never making it yours,

01:24:58.290 --> 01:25:00.903
that's the part of self-knowledge.

01:25:01.950 --> 01:25:05.640
The digestion of this
collective information

01:25:05.640 --> 01:25:09.600
until it becomes digested and
becomes part of yourselves,

01:25:09.600 --> 01:25:14.190
and then you are it
versus you are doing it.

01:25:14.190 --> 01:25:17.460
And this is a clear separation
that you can see in sports

01:25:17.460 --> 01:25:21.120
on a very high level and
on a not so high level,

01:25:21.120 --> 01:25:24.180
even though I would be honest

01:25:24.180 --> 01:25:27.450
if I say that some people reach very far

01:25:27.450 --> 01:25:28.920
just with collective knowledge

01:25:28.920 --> 01:25:31.290
and a very technical approach,

01:25:31.290 --> 01:25:36.290
and others reach extremely
far with very little of it.

01:25:36.390 --> 01:25:38.430
And there is always outliers,

01:25:38.430 --> 01:25:41.070
there are always the
outliers in that case.

01:25:41.070 --> 01:25:45.033
Another thought I had when
you mentioned evo-devo,

01:25:45.930 --> 01:25:49.620
evolution development is
also the Greek concepts

01:25:49.620 --> 01:25:53.913
of poiesis and physis.

01:25:55.050 --> 01:25:58.803
The growing of the seed into the tree,

01:26:01.050 --> 01:26:02.100
and the other process

01:26:02.100 --> 01:26:06.153
of the manufacturing of
the chair from the tree.

01:26:07.890 --> 01:26:12.890
Two processes of development
evolution, very different.

01:26:13.110 --> 01:26:15.870
One from everything to something,

01:26:15.870 --> 01:26:18.240
the other from nothing to something.

01:26:18.240 --> 01:26:22.053
One is accumulation based,
one is subtraction based.

01:26:23.190 --> 01:26:26.250
Both of these processes relate
to collective knowledge,

01:26:26.250 --> 01:26:29.640
self-knowledge, but they're
not exactly just that.

01:26:29.640 --> 01:26:31.530
And what should we do?

01:26:31.530 --> 01:26:34.230
This is a question that my friend Rasmus,

01:26:34.230 --> 01:26:38.310
he asks in his thesis and thoughts,

01:26:38.310 --> 01:26:40.650
what is the ultimate for us?

01:26:40.650 --> 01:26:43.530
Should we manufacture our chair

01:26:43.530 --> 01:26:46.950
or should we grow like into the tree?

01:26:46.950 --> 01:26:49.380
Civilize the mind, leave savage the body.

01:26:49.380 --> 01:26:54.380
Is it in this way or should
the mind also be left wild?

01:26:58.170 --> 01:27:01.697
Wild and wise is a nice
combination of words

01:27:01.697 --> 01:27:05.130
that I like to place together, wild, wise.

01:27:05.130 --> 01:27:08.580
So, this is something that I try to bring

01:27:08.580 --> 01:27:10.980
into the way that I live
my life and my practice.

01:27:10.980 --> 01:27:15.150
And I try to bring the information,

01:27:15.150 --> 01:27:20.150
and the wisdom, and the
collective knowledge,

01:27:20.550 --> 01:27:24.150
but I also try to let go of more and more

01:27:24.150 --> 01:27:25.920
until an essence is gleaned,

01:27:25.920 --> 01:27:28.440
until something is appearing

01:27:28.440 --> 01:27:31.080
and because everything was already there.

01:27:31.080 --> 01:27:34.293
For example, if I'm sitting here,

01:27:35.730 --> 01:27:40.653
all the movements are already occurring,

01:27:41.730 --> 01:27:44.910
all the possibilities are.

01:27:44.910 --> 01:27:47.610
So, it's just about, I need to open,

01:27:47.610 --> 01:27:49.890
I open this window, the
air would come from here.

01:27:49.890 --> 01:27:51.570
If I open this window, the air would come.

01:27:51.570 --> 01:27:53.580
I don't need to drive my motion,

01:27:53.580 --> 01:27:56.523
I need to discover what is
stopping it from happening.

01:27:57.630 --> 01:27:59.700
Something is constantly holding,

01:27:59.700 --> 01:28:03.180
and when we remove this
immediately movement appears,

01:28:03.180 --> 01:28:05.310
this is real deep movement

01:28:05.310 --> 01:28:08.760
versus the driven movement

01:28:08.760 --> 01:28:11.400
that is a very wasteful
of times like walking,

01:28:11.400 --> 01:28:13.290
you see people pushing through the walk

01:28:13.290 --> 01:28:17.073
instead of the controlled
falling that it should be.

01:28:18.360 --> 01:28:23.360
Fighting, punching to
manufacture the strength

01:28:24.030 --> 01:28:27.030
and then to have someone who knows

01:28:27.030 --> 01:28:29.880
how to facilitate the conditions

01:28:29.880 --> 01:28:32.490
in which you are knocked out.

01:28:32.490 --> 01:28:34.117
It doesn't knock you out,

01:28:34.117 --> 01:28:38.250
"It hits versus I hit,"
like Bruce Lee said.

01:28:38.250 --> 01:28:40.797
So, this is a beautiful thing to examine

01:28:40.797 --> 01:28:42.360
and to work within that,

01:28:42.360 --> 01:28:46.530
so it's to see am I skateboarding?

01:28:46.530 --> 01:28:48.303
Am I using this perspective?

01:28:49.200 --> 01:28:53.220
Or am I trying to, to control
because of risk and danger,

01:28:53.220 --> 01:28:55.050
I'm trying to overly control something

01:28:55.050 --> 01:28:57.210
that actually can never be controlled.

01:28:57.210 --> 01:29:00.270
The way to control it is
to let go of the control

01:29:00.270 --> 01:29:03.810
and then, okay, but what
about all this collection

01:29:03.810 --> 01:29:06.850
of information, knowledge
that I can bring in?

01:29:06.850 --> 01:29:08.880
Where do I want to play?

01:29:08.880 --> 01:29:12.000
I can play down here
or I can play up here.

01:29:12.000 --> 01:29:15.270
The collective knowledge is
maybe take you further in,

01:29:15.270 --> 01:29:18.090
and then you're still going
need to do your individual work.

01:29:18.090 --> 01:29:20.100
A lot of people like
to romanticize on that

01:29:20.100 --> 01:29:24.300
and it's you don't need
teachers, we don't need nothing,

01:29:24.300 --> 01:29:25.890
we don't need information.

01:29:25.890 --> 01:29:28.620
It's not fully honest, you don't need,

01:29:28.620 --> 01:29:31.140
but depends on where you want to function

01:29:31.140 --> 01:29:32.087
and how you want to function.

01:29:32.087 --> 01:29:33.840
They shouldn't be demonized,

01:29:33.840 --> 01:29:37.020
but they shouldn't be
overly glorified as well.

01:29:37.020 --> 01:29:39.570
- You mentioned about the
opportunity for movement,

01:29:39.570 --> 01:29:42.600
perhaps even all forms of
movement coming from deep within,

01:29:42.600 --> 01:29:46.800
it raises to mind in the
neuroscience of motor systems

01:29:46.800 --> 01:29:49.110
we talk about motor
neurons, as I described,

01:29:49.110 --> 01:29:52.710
the ones that actually evoke
contraction of muscles.

01:29:52.710 --> 01:29:55.020
And then, there's this category of neurons

01:29:55.020 --> 01:29:58.113
that isn't often discussed,
but certainly exist.

01:29:59.190 --> 01:30:00.240
Aren't often discussed

01:30:00.240 --> 01:30:02.100
in kind of popular
nomenclature of neuroscience,

01:30:02.100 --> 01:30:04.500
which is the pre-motor system.

01:30:04.500 --> 01:30:07.350
Most of our movements are the reflection

01:30:07.350 --> 01:30:11.100
of certain patterns of transmission

01:30:11.100 --> 01:30:14.880
breaking through from the
pre-motor to the actual motor.

01:30:14.880 --> 01:30:16.890
In other words, we are always

01:30:16.890 --> 01:30:20.940
in a anticipatory mode of movement.

01:30:20.940 --> 01:30:23.610
And as and I think you,
the way you describe it,

01:30:23.610 --> 01:30:25.410
you clearly intuitively understand this,

01:30:25.410 --> 01:30:27.300
you feel it, and you recognize it.

01:30:27.300 --> 01:30:30.120
And think of it as it's
like a layer of neurons

01:30:30.120 --> 01:30:31.701
that's constantly humming

01:30:31.701 --> 01:30:33.390
[hums] ready to go.

01:30:33.390 --> 01:30:35.070
And it's the release of these gates

01:30:35.070 --> 01:30:37.800
that allows movement to
occur in a particular way,

01:30:37.800 --> 01:30:40.350
could be very smooth,
could be very ballistic.

01:30:40.350 --> 01:30:43.080
- Which is DNA, the same,

01:30:43.080 --> 01:30:46.560
turning off and on, all the
information is already there.

01:30:46.560 --> 01:30:47.847
- Right.
- And then the possibilities

01:30:47.847 --> 01:30:49.500
are just allowed.

01:30:49.500 --> 01:30:50.910
So, I'm allowed,

01:30:50.910 --> 01:30:55.470
I don't do free will already,

01:30:55.470 --> 01:30:59.610
but I am allowed to do, I am.

01:30:59.610 --> 01:31:01.650
There are possibilities

01:31:01.650 --> 01:31:06.650
and I am dancing within that dance,

01:31:07.530 --> 01:31:09.393
but I am not the only dancer.

01:31:10.350 --> 01:31:12.690
So, that's, that's my sensation

01:31:12.690 --> 01:31:15.790
at least with most

01:31:18.180 --> 01:31:20.190
states of being, let's say.

01:31:20.190 --> 01:31:25.190
Maybe there is other states
that could be reached,

01:31:25.860 --> 01:31:28.980
a stability that will
arrive from the waters,

01:31:28.980 --> 01:31:31.170
from the movement of the waters,

01:31:31.170 --> 01:31:35.670
this humming, these
potential possibilities

01:31:35.670 --> 01:31:37.440
to be in that state,

01:31:37.440 --> 01:31:41.400
to vibrate like this is
very powerful for our lives.

01:31:41.400 --> 01:31:45.180
To wake up in the morning
and feel that living thing

01:31:45.180 --> 01:31:46.473
is the feeling of movement,

01:31:46.473 --> 01:31:48.990
and, for me, it's a
result of the practice.

01:31:48.990 --> 01:31:53.070
And so, then it's easy not to stagnate

01:31:53.070 --> 01:31:55.440
and then the mind can
stay focused for hours

01:31:55.440 --> 01:31:56.790
like we've done today,

01:31:56.790 --> 01:32:01.110
and I can listen and tune
in and I won't lose you.

01:32:01.110 --> 01:32:02.010
Which is very difficult,

01:32:02.010 --> 01:32:03.840
like I haven't had a good conversation

01:32:03.840 --> 01:32:06.450
here in the US it's very difficult,

01:32:06.450 --> 01:32:09.090
and I've had your attention
and you're listening,

01:32:09.090 --> 01:32:10.320
but it's rare.

01:32:10.320 --> 01:32:11.850
It's rare that somebody can do that,

01:32:11.850 --> 01:32:14.610
and it's a struggle, always a struggle,

01:32:14.610 --> 01:32:18.480
but it's definitely my
trick, my dirty trick.

01:32:18.480 --> 01:32:21.030
- In that you said you're allowed

01:32:21.030 --> 01:32:22.980
and, again, I'm taking
some of the language

01:32:22.980 --> 01:32:25.320
and what you report about your experience

01:32:25.320 --> 01:32:27.960
and I'm trying to map it to some concepts

01:32:27.960 --> 01:32:29.670
that relate to neural circuits.

01:32:29.670 --> 01:32:32.520
In the principles of neuroscience,

01:32:32.520 --> 01:32:36.810
we talk about instructiveness
versus permissiveness.

01:32:36.810 --> 01:32:38.160
There are instructive cues,

01:32:38.160 --> 01:32:41.190
like for instance, the ability
to pick up this pen, right?

01:32:41.190 --> 01:32:45.000
There's an instruction, clearly
there's a motor command,

01:32:45.000 --> 01:32:46.830
but that's just one way of looking at it.

01:32:46.830 --> 01:32:48.000
The way it actually works

01:32:48.000 --> 01:32:49.770
is that there's a pre-motor system

01:32:49.770 --> 01:32:51.810
that's already generating that movement,

01:32:51.810 --> 01:32:53.730
and what we've done is
we've flung open the gate

01:32:53.730 --> 01:32:56.070
and allowed that movement
to occur precisely.

01:32:56.070 --> 01:32:57.780
- Surfing it.

01:32:57.780 --> 01:32:58.793
- Right.
- Surfing that current,

01:32:58.793 --> 01:33:01.140
or this current, or another current,

01:33:01.140 --> 01:33:02.580
or opening the window.

01:33:02.580 --> 01:33:07.230
- Exactly, and if you
look at the formal study

01:33:07.230 --> 01:33:10.740
of movement and improvement of movement,

01:33:10.740 --> 01:33:14.280
the most basic example I can
give is like a tennis serve.

01:33:14.280 --> 01:33:15.117
And they, if you just,

01:33:15.117 --> 01:33:16.920
and they've done this many times over.

01:33:16.920 --> 01:33:18.090
You map the trajectories,

01:33:18.090 --> 01:33:21.090
and in a novice the lines
are all over the place,

01:33:21.090 --> 01:33:23.910
it ends up looking more like a tangle

01:33:23.910 --> 01:33:25.920
of rubber band ball, right?

01:33:25.920 --> 01:33:28.920
Whereas in the Federer or the expert,

01:33:28.920 --> 01:33:31.620
you almost wonder if it's
just one line being drawn,

01:33:31.620 --> 01:33:35.040
but it's the trajectories
are incredibly stereotyped.

01:33:35.040 --> 01:33:37.110
That's the reflection of
one little narrow gate

01:33:37.110 --> 01:33:39.240
opening again, and again, and again.

01:33:39.240 --> 01:33:41.100
Of course-
- Let me inject

01:33:41.100 --> 01:33:45.720
something here from an old
neurologist, you can say,

01:33:45.720 --> 01:33:48.946
Bernstein, the Soviet.

01:33:48.946 --> 01:33:51.570
And he talked about degrees of freedom,

01:33:51.570 --> 01:33:54.630
and they did in order
to increase productivity

01:33:54.630 --> 01:33:55.743
in Soviet Union.

01:33:57.360 --> 01:33:59.267
I don't know if you
you've heard this story.

01:33:59.267 --> 01:34:00.690
- [Andrew] No.

01:34:00.690 --> 01:34:03.630
- He was brought in to
examine the movement habits

01:34:03.630 --> 01:34:08.630
of the workers and he
collected some information.

01:34:09.390 --> 01:34:12.480
He placed, he was one
of the first kinetic,

01:34:12.480 --> 01:34:14.400
I don't know how it's called in English,

01:34:14.400 --> 01:34:17.610
the kinetic capturing of motion

01:34:17.610 --> 01:34:20.370
with moving pictures in that time.

01:34:20.370 --> 01:34:24.450
And so, he placed these dots
and they took these photos,

01:34:24.450 --> 01:34:26.760
which became kind of moving.

01:34:26.760 --> 01:34:30.210
And what he discovered was
something very interesting,

01:34:30.210 --> 01:34:34.150
the accuracy of the
heat of the sledgehammer

01:34:36.120 --> 01:34:40.900
increased while the variants
in the various points

01:34:41.940 --> 01:34:43.920
became more, not less.

01:34:43.920 --> 01:34:46.050
So, it wasn't a fixed pattern,

01:34:46.050 --> 01:34:47.523
it was a meta pattern.

01:34:48.480 --> 01:34:50.740
And this pattern is adjusted

01:34:51.630 --> 01:34:55.860
in this way to achieve
the perfect execution.

01:34:55.860 --> 01:34:57.570
Those were very early findings,

01:34:57.570 --> 01:35:00.750
and I'm not sure how does
that sit with everything,

01:35:00.750 --> 01:35:04.293
but I'm sure there is some
truth to it from my experience.

01:35:05.220 --> 01:35:09.360
Basically, the self-adjusting
dynamic nature of the system

01:35:09.360 --> 01:35:12.930
allows you to reach a very constant

01:35:12.930 --> 01:35:17.880
and stable end result by being so open

01:35:17.880 --> 01:35:19.320
and letting go of your control.

01:35:19.320 --> 01:35:22.980
- Mm-hmm, the example
you give fits very well

01:35:22.980 --> 01:35:24.750
with the one that I described before

01:35:24.750 --> 01:35:26.610
because and I'm recalling the experiment.

01:35:26.610 --> 01:35:28.260
If people want to look
this up, it's a paper,

01:35:28.260 --> 01:35:29.880
we'll put it in the show note caption,

01:35:29.880 --> 01:35:32.190
a guy also happens to be at Harvard

01:35:32.190 --> 01:35:34.410
named Bence Ölveczky, a Hungarian,

01:35:34.410 --> 01:35:35.880
I'm clearly pronouncing his name wrong,

01:35:35.880 --> 01:35:36.990
but I know Bence.

01:35:36.990 --> 01:35:39.900
And I remember the slide in my mind's eye,

01:35:39.900 --> 01:35:41.250
and the trajectory that was mapped

01:35:41.250 --> 01:35:42.660
was the movement of the tennis racket,

01:35:42.660 --> 01:35:45.660
not of the limbs themselves
in the Federer case.

01:35:45.660 --> 01:35:48.690
So, that I think aligns well
with what you're describing.

01:35:48.690 --> 01:35:51.270
Yeah, that exploration
of degrees of freedom

01:35:51.270 --> 01:35:52.770
is where the opportunity

01:35:52.770 --> 01:35:56.670
for real advancement

01:35:56.670 --> 01:35:58.230
and expansion of skill shows up.

01:35:58.230 --> 01:35:59.940
As that I think the way
it's been described to me

01:35:59.940 --> 01:36:03.330
is that we go from unskilled to skilled

01:36:03.330 --> 01:36:04.380
and then there's mastery,

01:36:04.380 --> 01:36:05.760
and then there's this top tier,

01:36:05.760 --> 01:36:07.980
which is this beautiful, thin layer

01:36:07.980 --> 01:36:10.680
that so few people occupy,
which is virtuosity,

01:36:10.680 --> 01:36:13.890
in which the practitioner
invites variability

01:36:13.890 --> 01:36:17.643
and chance back in as an
opportunity to do truly new things.

01:36:21.930 --> 01:36:24.300
- It made me think many years ago,

01:36:24.300 --> 01:36:28.380
this kind of thinking about
so what is that entity?

01:36:28.380 --> 01:36:30.720
Because obviously, it's not technique,

01:36:30.720 --> 01:36:32.070
and it wouldn't even be honest

01:36:32.070 --> 01:36:34.380
to say it's a movement pattern,

01:36:34.380 --> 01:36:37.170
there is too much diversity there.

01:36:37.170 --> 01:36:38.550
I started to talk about,

01:36:38.550 --> 01:36:42.120
I called it movement
sleeves or meta technique,

01:36:42.120 --> 01:36:46.323
but the word technique
is already misleading.

01:36:47.790 --> 01:36:51.450
So, there is some kind of a dynamic sleeve

01:36:51.450 --> 01:36:53.730
in which you can move in

01:36:53.730 --> 01:36:56.100
as long as you are not out of this sleeve,

01:36:56.100 --> 01:36:58.260
you are still within the boundaries

01:36:58.260 --> 01:37:01.770
of achieving the result that you're after.

01:37:01.770 --> 01:37:04.530
And then, the result is adaptation

01:37:04.530 --> 01:37:07.770
of all these elements inside
to keep you in the sleeve.

01:37:07.770 --> 01:37:11.287
The sleeve is not constricted
as we once thought,

01:37:11.287 --> 01:37:13.380
"Oh, beautiful technique."

01:37:13.380 --> 01:37:15.693
There are many ways to skin a cat,

01:37:17.070 --> 01:37:21.900
and that experience, and
that variety, that diversity

01:37:21.900 --> 01:37:23.460
goes into virtuosity.

01:37:23.460 --> 01:37:27.660
It's true freedom because your
focus is on the right thing.

01:37:27.660 --> 01:37:30.510
You don't point at the
moon, look at your finger,

01:37:30.510 --> 01:37:34.770
and that's really in
essence being a virtuoso,

01:37:34.770 --> 01:37:39.090
for me, like mastery, let's
say if there is such a thing.

01:37:39.090 --> 01:37:41.130
- Oh, there's I do believe
there is such a thing

01:37:41.130 --> 01:37:44.130
and I'll flatter and
attempt to embarrass you

01:37:44.130 --> 01:37:45.600
by saying I think that I'm not alone

01:37:45.600 --> 01:37:47.670
in viewing you as a virtuoso of movement.

01:37:47.670 --> 01:37:50.280
I think that's what comes to mind

01:37:50.280 --> 01:37:51.660
because there's this notion

01:37:51.660 --> 01:37:54.450
that not everything is pre-planned,

01:37:54.450 --> 01:37:56.190
that even you might not know

01:37:56.190 --> 01:37:59.040
what you're going to do next
until the moment of execution,

01:37:59.040 --> 01:38:02.583
but that here I'm projecting
my own assumptions.

01:38:05.370 --> 01:38:07.950
I'd like to talk about mindsets

01:38:07.950 --> 01:38:10.950
in approaching practice a little bit more,

01:38:10.950 --> 01:38:13.740
but I want to wade into that territory

01:38:13.740 --> 01:38:16.800
by talking about vision in the eyes,

01:38:16.800 --> 01:38:19.350
something that we both
share a deep interest in.

01:38:19.350 --> 01:38:21.570
I from the background
of visual neuroscience,

01:38:21.570 --> 01:38:22.950
but also from the realization

01:38:22.950 --> 01:38:25.530
that we have this incredible ability

01:38:25.530 --> 01:38:27.720
to adjust the aperture
of our visual window,

01:38:27.720 --> 01:38:31.440
we can focus very narrowly
and we can focus very broadly.

01:38:31.440 --> 01:38:33.630
So, something I encountered,
I think first as a child,

01:38:33.630 --> 01:38:35.640
realizing that I could spend all day

01:38:35.640 --> 01:38:38.610
watching ants play in a very fine domain

01:38:38.610 --> 01:38:39.930
and then look up and go inside

01:38:39.930 --> 01:38:43.050
and realize there's a whole
world and realizing, "Wow,

01:38:43.050 --> 01:38:44.940
I'll never be able to consume

01:38:44.940 --> 01:38:48.630
the full range of experiences
at any one moment."

01:38:48.630 --> 01:38:50.790
There are ants probably
in the corner of this room

01:38:50.790 --> 01:38:51.753
doing their thing.

01:38:52.830 --> 01:38:55.470
And so too our approach to movement

01:38:55.470 --> 01:38:58.140
can be, as you mentioned,
very big and dynamic

01:38:58.140 --> 01:38:59.970
in terms of the broad movements

01:38:59.970 --> 01:39:02.280
of our limbs or fine articulation.

01:39:02.280 --> 01:39:04.380
When you begin a practice

01:39:04.380 --> 01:39:06.630
and as you move through a practice,

01:39:06.630 --> 01:39:11.630
do you apply a regimented
way of focusing your vision?

01:39:12.840 --> 01:39:14.370
Are you in panoramic vision?

01:39:14.370 --> 01:39:16.680
Are you in a very narrow field of view?

01:39:16.680 --> 01:39:18.510
Or does it entirely depend?

01:39:18.510 --> 01:39:20.520
And for the person who's a true beginner,

01:39:20.520 --> 01:39:22.500
a true novice, like myself,

01:39:22.500 --> 01:39:25.803
how should I show up to
the practice with my eyes?

01:39:27.240 --> 01:39:29.880
- The eyes are a good starting point

01:39:29.880 --> 01:39:33.660
as you help a lot of people to understand.

01:39:33.660 --> 01:39:37.320
And when you encounter
difficulties with other layers,

01:39:37.320 --> 01:39:39.990
it's very powerful to start with the eyes.

01:39:39.990 --> 01:39:43.050
Another thing important to understand

01:39:43.050 --> 01:39:46.260
and to experience, you can't believe me,

01:39:46.260 --> 01:39:49.650
or you've got to examine it for yourself,

01:39:49.650 --> 01:39:54.480
we do not move the eyes
as well as we think we do.

01:39:54.480 --> 01:39:57.660
'Cause as long as you can
see and move the eyes,

01:39:57.660 --> 01:40:01.230
people never think about
it, that it can be trained,

01:40:01.230 --> 01:40:03.360
that it can be improved, et cetera,

01:40:03.360 --> 01:40:06.303
and the effects of it are far reaching.

01:40:07.590 --> 01:40:09.990
The eyes lead to the inner eye,

01:40:09.990 --> 01:40:13.023
you can think of it in a
beautiful metaphorical way.

01:40:15.480 --> 01:40:18.483
And it's a representation of the way

01:40:18.483 --> 01:40:23.200
that we use various
cognitive and mind processes

01:40:24.060 --> 01:40:25.923
and also, of course, affect the body.

01:40:26.880 --> 01:40:30.570
The eyes lead in many ways

01:40:30.570 --> 01:40:34.860
and the head is also a very
because all of these inputs

01:40:34.860 --> 01:40:36.510
are coming in here.

01:40:36.510 --> 01:40:38.550
So, it's very easy to lead the body

01:40:38.550 --> 01:40:41.580
and if you look at it
centrally from the head,

01:40:41.580 --> 01:40:43.560
it's a very powerful and easy thing.

01:40:43.560 --> 01:40:47.913
For example, when you
teach boxers how to bob,

01:40:49.170 --> 01:40:50.250
usually it's not done

01:40:50.250 --> 01:40:53.910
in the way that I believe
it should be done,

01:40:53.910 --> 01:40:56.340
you teach it with the periphery.

01:40:56.340 --> 01:40:57.690
They teach it from the feet

01:40:58.590 --> 01:41:00.570
because they have the
idea, which is correct,

01:41:00.570 --> 01:41:04.470
that you need to do it
in spatial conditions,

01:41:04.470 --> 01:41:07.320
in movement, in space.

01:41:07.320 --> 01:41:10.120
But in reality, the head will
organize the feet for you.

01:41:11.460 --> 01:41:14.100
Instead, you are now putting
two elements together

01:41:14.100 --> 01:41:15.630
and then with years of practice,

01:41:15.630 --> 01:41:18.150
you hope of tying them together well.

01:41:18.150 --> 01:41:19.500
I prefer to do something else

01:41:19.500 --> 01:41:21.663
because if I'll pull
your head now to side,

01:41:22.650 --> 01:41:25.980
you will immediately start to
organize your feet under you.

01:41:25.980 --> 01:41:27.540
So, I give you just one element

01:41:27.540 --> 01:41:28.890
to manipulate the system from,

01:41:28.890 --> 01:41:32.160
that's how I would teach
someone something like this.

01:41:32.160 --> 01:41:34.320
Many animals hunt with the head,

01:41:34.320 --> 01:41:36.450
so you can see the body running forward

01:41:36.450 --> 01:41:38.220
while the head is turning to the side,

01:41:38.220 --> 01:41:40.290
the whole thing follows afterwards.

01:41:40.290 --> 01:41:42.480
So, it's a very powerful
way to address movement,

01:41:42.480 --> 01:41:43.503
not the only one,

01:41:44.460 --> 01:41:46.020
there are many modes, thankfully,

01:41:46.020 --> 01:41:47.670
and we're very adaptable in that,

01:41:47.670 --> 01:41:50.280
but definitely a primary one.

01:41:50.280 --> 01:41:52.590
And then, the use of
the eyes is, of course,

01:41:52.590 --> 01:41:55.983
maybe the most important
element with that usually.

01:41:58.950 --> 01:42:01.500
Yeah, what else can I say about the eyes?

01:42:01.500 --> 01:42:03.030
So, how do you come in?

01:42:03.030 --> 01:42:04.530
Well, it depends on the practice,

01:42:04.530 --> 01:42:08.430
you need to start to have
some kind of a checklist

01:42:08.430 --> 01:42:11.220
of what you're looking to do.

01:42:11.220 --> 01:42:12.090
And then by this,

01:42:12.090 --> 01:42:15.900
you can start to tailor the
way that you use your eyes.

01:42:15.900 --> 01:42:18.300
The same thing I do for posture,

01:42:18.300 --> 01:42:20.160
the same thing I do for stance,

01:42:20.160 --> 01:42:22.503
the same thing eventually I do for state.

01:42:23.610 --> 01:42:25.050
And there is different flavors,

01:42:25.050 --> 01:42:26.850
there is no correct way to use the eyes.

01:42:26.850 --> 01:42:28.530
Sometimes it's very peripheral,

01:42:28.530 --> 01:42:31.860
soft, open awareness orientation,

01:42:31.860 --> 01:42:33.693
sometimes it's very focused.

01:42:34.620 --> 01:42:38.370
Notice that I'm pulling
these two opposites,

01:42:38.370 --> 01:42:39.840
awareness and focus,

01:42:39.840 --> 01:42:42.570
which is often put together and fused,

01:42:42.570 --> 01:42:46.020
but and then the eyes

01:42:46.020 --> 01:42:47.880
are like the immediate

01:42:47.880 --> 01:42:51.330
and the easiest entry point into that.

01:42:51.330 --> 01:42:54.753
Another thing is the placement
of the head and the eyes,

01:42:55.950 --> 01:42:59.130
like for example, when we lower our chin,

01:42:59.130 --> 01:43:01.593
we seem to see better.

01:43:02.520 --> 01:43:04.890
When we raise the eyebrows,

01:43:04.890 --> 01:43:08.310
there is too much exposure
of top light sources

01:43:08.310 --> 01:43:11.160
and so people would usually
when looking into the distance

01:43:11.160 --> 01:43:13.380
will tilt their chin in.

01:43:13.380 --> 01:43:17.160
And in many scenarios, tilting
of the chin to the side,

01:43:17.160 --> 01:43:20.850
or placing just like
listening with the ear,

01:43:20.850 --> 01:43:23.730
placing a certain eye or dominant eye,

01:43:23.730 --> 01:43:26.490
depending on various scenarios.

01:43:26.490 --> 01:43:28.200
And this is all like information

01:43:28.200 --> 01:43:31.200
that I can come in cerebrally,

01:43:31.200 --> 01:43:34.710
and think about, and
jump my practice forward.

01:43:34.710 --> 01:43:38.520
Instead, of just letting the
experience teach me that,

01:43:38.520 --> 01:43:41.340
I'm using some kind of a
thinking process to improve.

01:43:41.340 --> 01:43:44.430
And this is not cheating, this is great.

01:43:44.430 --> 01:43:45.450
Will it work?

01:43:45.450 --> 01:43:48.690
We got to try, it's a process.

01:43:48.690 --> 01:43:51.630
And those are some thoughts
and to start to play with.

01:43:51.630 --> 01:43:56.070
- Yeah, I love that
you mentioned chin down

01:43:56.070 --> 01:43:57.750
because we all have a natural reflex

01:43:57.750 --> 01:43:59.400
when chin goes down, eyes goes up,

01:43:59.400 --> 01:44:03.300
and the opposite is true when
head goes up, eyes go down.

01:44:03.300 --> 01:44:05.580
And there are two separate
clusters of neurons

01:44:05.580 --> 01:44:09.273
in these cranial nerve
nuclei that, as we call them,

01:44:10.170 --> 01:44:11.493
when eyes are up,

01:44:12.450 --> 01:44:14.949
it increases our level
of alertness overall.

01:44:14.949 --> 01:44:18.030
This is not woo science,

01:44:18.030 --> 01:44:22.110
this is the function of
these cranial nerve nuclei.

01:44:22.110 --> 01:44:24.540
When our eyes are down,

01:44:24.540 --> 01:44:27.300
we go into states of
more calm and quiescence.

01:44:27.300 --> 01:44:28.380
And this makes perfect sense,

01:44:28.380 --> 01:44:30.000
you know, and then the
eyelids usually go down

01:44:30.000 --> 01:44:31.590
and then people fall asleep.

01:44:31.590 --> 01:44:33.690
Eyes up does not mean head up

01:44:33.690 --> 01:44:35.850
'cause, as you said, there's
a very dynamic control

01:44:35.850 --> 01:44:38.430
over the amount of luminance
depending on the environment.

01:44:38.430 --> 01:44:41.340
So, that and then, as you mentioned,

01:44:41.340 --> 01:44:43.890
this difference between
focus and awareness,

01:44:43.890 --> 01:44:46.170
I think is a really important one.

01:44:46.170 --> 01:44:48.880
When we are in this more
panoramic, soft gaze

01:44:49.770 --> 01:44:53.850
and broad awareness, big swaths
of visual field, as we say,

01:44:53.850 --> 01:44:55.440
the neurons that control that

01:44:55.440 --> 01:44:57.480
come through a pathway
called magnocellular pathway.

01:44:57.480 --> 01:44:59.520
In any event, those
neurons are much thicker,

01:44:59.520 --> 01:45:01.470
thicker cables, they transmit much faster,

01:45:01.470 --> 01:45:05.580
just like thick pipes can
carry more water more quickly.

01:45:05.580 --> 01:45:09.300
And your reaction time
is at least four times

01:45:09.300 --> 01:45:11.760
what it is in this awareness mode

01:45:11.760 --> 01:45:14.130
than it is when you're
narrowly focused on something.

01:45:14.130 --> 01:45:16.470
And this is counterintuitive,
I think, to a lot of people,

01:45:16.470 --> 01:45:18.270
but the person who is
running to catch the ball

01:45:18.270 --> 01:45:21.232
is not tracking the ball
in a smooth movement,

01:45:21.232 --> 01:45:23.520
most of their vision is
in peripheral vision.

01:45:23.520 --> 01:45:25.770
When we drive we're in
this peripheral vision

01:45:25.770 --> 01:45:28.530
and our reaction times
are much, much faster.

01:45:28.530 --> 01:45:32.730
So, I don't know if I'm
reluctant to encourage people

01:45:32.730 --> 01:45:34.980
to shift toward a
particular type of practice

01:45:34.980 --> 01:45:36.150
toward a particular type of vision.

01:45:36.150 --> 01:45:37.539
I think what you and I,

01:45:37.539 --> 01:45:41.160
I hope agree on, correct me if I'm wrong,

01:45:41.160 --> 01:45:44.610
is that exploring these different extremes

01:45:44.610 --> 01:45:46.950
and everything in between
is where the real value is.

01:45:46.950 --> 01:45:49.230
Panoramic focused,

01:45:49.230 --> 01:45:51.210
eyes, head up, eyes down,

01:45:51.210 --> 01:45:52.260
head down, eyes up,

01:45:52.260 --> 01:45:54.600
playing with it and exploring it

01:45:54.600 --> 01:45:58.860
and as opposed to for the
first 10 minutes of practice

01:45:58.860 --> 01:46:00.060
being panoramic vision.

01:46:00.060 --> 01:46:00.893
You know, the sort of-

01:46:00.893 --> 01:46:02.670
- Yeah.
- earlier today we were joking

01:46:02.670 --> 01:46:05.400
about and kind of lamenting the fact

01:46:05.400 --> 01:46:07.470
that this word biohacking exists

01:46:07.470 --> 01:46:09.420
or that the optimal performance,

01:46:09.420 --> 01:46:11.190
it's that they're unfortunate terms

01:46:11.190 --> 01:46:13.560
because they suggest that
if you just plug it in,

01:46:13.560 --> 01:46:15.150
it's going to be like
two plus two equals four

01:46:15.150 --> 01:46:17.610
and you're going to get
it right every time.

01:46:17.610 --> 01:46:20.400
- Another pragmatic bit
here, if I can offer,

01:46:20.400 --> 01:46:25.400
is since our culture

01:46:25.740 --> 01:46:29.021
has been more geared and
pushing us towards focus,

01:46:29.021 --> 01:46:30.450
the focus use of the eyes,

01:46:30.450 --> 01:46:34.710
and primary language,
reading, and other things,

01:46:34.710 --> 01:46:36.600
we have less opportunities

01:46:36.600 --> 01:46:39.630
to work with the more open panoramic one.

01:46:39.630 --> 01:46:41.520
So, it would be smart

01:46:41.520 --> 01:46:45.570
to start to balance things out a bit more.

01:46:45.570 --> 01:46:48.810
When you're in nature, you
don't look at each leaf,

01:46:48.810 --> 01:46:52.320
everything is moving, and you
are kind of immersed in that.

01:46:52.320 --> 01:46:53.917
And then, something
attracts your attention,

01:46:53.917 --> 01:46:55.800
"Oh, it's a bird," and you focus,

01:46:55.800 --> 01:46:59.190
and you go back into the general state,

01:46:59.190 --> 01:47:02.790
the base state, which is open awareness.

01:47:02.790 --> 01:47:05.820
Here, we switch things
around in our modern culture,

01:47:05.820 --> 01:47:10.110
we are mostly focused and
then we sometimes daydream,

01:47:10.110 --> 01:47:11.914
which is maybe some kind

01:47:11.914 --> 01:47:16.020
of a balancing act

01:47:16.020 --> 01:47:17.520
that comes from deep within,

01:47:17.520 --> 01:47:20.100
I don't know, maybe you can share

01:47:20.100 --> 01:47:21.300
some information about that,

01:47:21.300 --> 01:47:24.450
but I see that many time people need to,

01:47:24.450 --> 01:47:29.450
the focus is overly done
by far in our lives.

01:47:29.490 --> 01:47:30.840
- I couldn't agree more,

01:47:30.840 --> 01:47:33.390
and I think a lot of I'll
even venture so far as to say

01:47:33.390 --> 01:47:36.450
that a lot of the visual deficits

01:47:36.450 --> 01:47:38.340
that we now see in young people,

01:47:38.340 --> 01:47:41.400
myopia, literally nearsightedness, occurs

01:47:41.400 --> 01:47:43.560
because if we look at things
that are too close to us

01:47:43.560 --> 01:47:44.820
as children or as adults,

01:47:44.820 --> 01:47:46.830
the eyeball actually gets longer.

01:47:46.830 --> 01:47:50.820
The lens focuses the visual image in front

01:47:50.820 --> 01:47:53.280
of nearer to the lens, nearsighted,

01:47:53.280 --> 01:47:56.760
than in front of where it should land.

01:47:56.760 --> 01:48:01.170
And basically, it's a
lack of panoramic vision

01:48:01.170 --> 01:48:04.590
that is or open awareness
that's driving these changes.

01:48:04.590 --> 01:48:06.870
And nowadays, we are essentially,

01:48:06.870 --> 01:48:11.040
most people are 90% of the
time in this narrow focus mode.

01:48:11.040 --> 01:48:12.090
You know, right before recording,

01:48:12.090 --> 01:48:14.400
we took a break and went
up to look at a vista

01:48:14.400 --> 01:48:18.300
and to look off to the
distance, incredibly useful,

01:48:18.300 --> 01:48:19.740
easy practice at some level,

01:48:19.740 --> 01:48:22.410
but I think most people are
not doing this sort of thing.

01:48:22.410 --> 01:48:23.970
And the way that it shapes the mind

01:48:23.970 --> 01:48:25.230
and the perception of time,

01:48:25.230 --> 01:48:28.350
of course, is a whole
other kingdom of ideas.

01:48:28.350 --> 01:48:31.290
But one thing I'd like to relate

01:48:31.290 --> 01:48:33.690
this element of vision
to and open awareness

01:48:33.690 --> 01:48:36.540
is earlier you mentioned the
cone of auditory attention,

01:48:36.540 --> 01:48:38.880
the other sense that we can play with

01:48:38.880 --> 01:48:42.423
in as in our practice
and throughout the day.

01:48:43.650 --> 01:48:47.610
Do you see any value to both
paying attention to things

01:48:47.610 --> 01:48:49.620
in a very narrow cone
of auditory attention,

01:48:49.620 --> 01:48:51.900
but also just walking and listening

01:48:51.900 --> 01:48:53.700
to all the sounds at once?

01:48:53.700 --> 01:48:55.380
I could imagine that could be useful.

01:48:55.380 --> 01:48:58.650
And in terms of physical
movement practices,

01:48:58.650 --> 01:49:00.240
I was going to say where are your ears?

01:49:00.240 --> 01:49:02.100
Your ears are always more
or less in the same place,

01:49:02.100 --> 01:49:06.238
but where is your hearing when
you approach your practice?

01:49:06.238 --> 01:49:09.660
- Another set of
parameters to think about,

01:49:09.660 --> 01:49:11.673
and to play with, and to be aware of.

01:49:13.530 --> 01:49:16.950
Also, I have the
experience that some people

01:49:16.950 --> 01:49:21.950
are better at using this
system or that system.

01:49:22.500 --> 01:49:27.500
And you would be amazed how
differently the same results

01:49:27.690 --> 01:49:29.580
seemingly outside results are done

01:49:29.580 --> 01:49:32.283
by different practitioners
and different scenarios.

01:49:35.100 --> 01:49:37.620
This goes into this
mutation and change idea

01:49:37.620 --> 01:49:40.890
is what really jumps us forward eventually

01:49:40.890 --> 01:49:42.750
some kind of a mutation.

01:49:42.750 --> 01:49:47.340
So, it's like all of our culture,
and practices, and success

01:49:47.340 --> 01:49:50.160
puts us closer and closer to each other,

01:49:50.160 --> 01:49:54.420
so we have the same opinions
everywhere around the world

01:49:54.420 --> 01:49:56.940
becoming more and more the same,

01:49:56.940 --> 01:49:58.650
less and less different,

01:49:58.650 --> 01:50:02.490
but the real hope comes
from the different,

01:50:02.490 --> 01:50:07.490
so and we have a difficulty
in promoting that.

01:50:09.570 --> 01:50:13.380
And so, this is another
thing that can be promoted

01:50:13.380 --> 01:50:15.540
with the right practices,

01:50:15.540 --> 01:50:19.290
the right, for example,
I work with corporates

01:50:19.290 --> 01:50:22.110
or even worked with governments before

01:50:22.110 --> 01:50:24.090
to bring in some of that freshness

01:50:24.090 --> 01:50:26.610
with simple habits in the workday,

01:50:26.610 --> 01:50:28.890
or in the education of children,

01:50:28.890 --> 01:50:33.890
or in companies increasing productivity.

01:50:34.740 --> 01:50:36.720
I don't really give a fuck,

01:50:36.720 --> 01:50:41.340
but I am there to give
what I view is important

01:50:41.340 --> 01:50:45.123
and what is important maybe
increases productivity,

01:50:46.020 --> 01:50:47.220
but it's more important to me

01:50:47.220 --> 01:50:50.430
that it improves people's
lives who are involved

01:50:50.430 --> 01:50:55.430
and improves, yeah, being and becoming,

01:50:56.100 --> 01:50:59.190
being and becoming, these two entities.

01:50:59.190 --> 01:51:02.910
I'm not there, I'm on
my way, I'm a process.

01:51:02.910 --> 01:51:06.750
So, thinking about hearing,

01:51:06.750 --> 01:51:09.060
the way that people use their ears,

01:51:09.060 --> 01:51:10.953
the way that people use listening.

01:51:12.210 --> 01:51:15.543
Again, we can talk about
placement of the head and posture,

01:51:17.010 --> 01:51:20.700
sometimes angling as well,
sharper angle, chin down,

01:51:20.700 --> 01:51:24.810
some people tend to use
the shape of the ear,

01:51:24.810 --> 01:51:28.890
people with different ears
closer or further out.

01:51:28.890 --> 01:51:31.500
This is it's if you're very sensitive

01:51:31.500 --> 01:51:32.370
and you're looking around,

01:51:32.370 --> 01:51:37.260
you would see this is
affecting people's motion.

01:51:37.260 --> 01:51:39.333
Even the shape of our face,

01:51:40.440 --> 01:51:42.270
like the development of the vocal chords

01:51:42.270 --> 01:51:46.320
and speaking will totally
change how we are, how we look,

01:51:46.320 --> 01:51:51.240
but how we listen also will do the same.

01:51:51.240 --> 01:51:52.560
I don't have any proof of it,

01:51:52.560 --> 01:51:55.773
but it is something I believe in.

01:51:56.624 --> 01:51:59.160
- Well, people will even make
their ears bigger, right?

01:51:59.160 --> 01:52:02.340
We, we try and become like
little fennec foxes or something

01:52:02.340 --> 01:52:04.200
by, I mean, a lot of people don't realize

01:52:04.200 --> 01:52:05.180
that's actually why we do this

01:52:05.180 --> 01:52:07.410
is to capture more sound waves, right?

01:52:07.410 --> 01:52:09.722
And the leaning is that
the localization of sound

01:52:09.722 --> 01:52:12.240
is based on a simple brainstem calculation

01:52:12.240 --> 01:52:14.370
of interaural time differences,

01:52:14.370 --> 01:52:16.350
the time in which something,

01:52:16.350 --> 01:52:19.080
the brain intuitively, it just knows

01:52:19.080 --> 01:52:20.910
'cause it's a pretty hardwired circuit

01:52:20.910 --> 01:52:23.460
that if a sound arrives first
to this ear then that ear,

01:52:23.460 --> 01:52:26.550
that it's likely coming from over here.

01:52:26.550 --> 01:52:28.230
Whereas, if it's dead center,

01:52:28.230 --> 01:52:29.850
arrives at the two at the same time,

01:52:29.850 --> 01:52:33.450
that it's almost ridiculously
simple when one hears it,

01:52:33.450 --> 01:52:34.530
no pun intended,

01:52:34.530 --> 01:52:39.530
but it is an incredibly
valuable way of thinking

01:52:39.690 --> 01:52:42.570
about how the architecture of the body

01:52:42.570 --> 01:52:44.163
changes our experience.

01:52:45.300 --> 01:52:47.370
Along those lines earlier,
you mentioned something

01:52:47.370 --> 01:52:50.463
and it flagged an
important question for me.

01:52:51.930 --> 01:52:55.192
When I see people walking

01:52:55.192 --> 01:52:59.310
sometimes I think, "Wow, they
really move in a strange way."

01:52:59.310 --> 01:53:00.330
Occasionally you see somebody,

01:53:00.330 --> 01:53:03.930
they walk really, it's
impressive for whatever reason,

01:53:03.930 --> 01:53:04.950
you know, and you just think, "Wow,

01:53:04.950 --> 01:53:06.447
they sort of glide along."

01:53:07.530 --> 01:53:09.330
People come in different shapes and sizes,

01:53:09.330 --> 01:53:11.733
short torsos, long arms, et cetera.

01:53:13.110 --> 01:53:16.260
Do you think that if
people have a body type

01:53:16.260 --> 01:53:19.350
that facilitates certain kinds
of movement and not others

01:53:19.350 --> 01:53:22.860
that they should intentionally
try and move in the way

01:53:22.860 --> 01:53:24.600
that is right at the edge

01:53:24.600 --> 01:53:27.810
of the kind of friction and challenge

01:53:27.810 --> 01:53:31.830
in order to shape new possibilities,

01:53:31.830 --> 01:53:33.150
or do you think that they should lean

01:53:33.150 --> 01:53:34.890
into the smooth execution

01:53:34.890 --> 01:53:36.740
of what comes most naturally to them?

01:53:37.710 --> 01:53:41.740
- Yeah, I think a good
practice is to have many walks

01:53:45.900 --> 01:53:47.040
because they're required.

01:53:47.040 --> 01:53:49.860
And, of course, there is a very efficient

01:53:49.860 --> 01:53:52.830
and endurance, stamina-oriented thing

01:53:52.830 --> 01:53:54.960
that if you have the experience,

01:53:54.960 --> 01:53:57.600
it will naturally develops and unravel.

01:53:57.600 --> 01:53:58.433
And if not,

01:53:58.433 --> 01:54:01.920
you can get some collective
knowledge and improve.

01:54:01.920 --> 01:54:05.520
And then there is a lot of emotion,

01:54:05.520 --> 01:54:07.500
emotional things related to walk

01:54:07.500 --> 01:54:10.330
like how I'm walking
into a business meeting

01:54:11.940 --> 01:54:14.830
and/or how I'm walking

01:54:16.290 --> 01:54:18.063
out of a bad situation.

01:54:19.470 --> 01:54:24.270
And there is a lot of beautiful
things to research there

01:54:24.270 --> 01:54:25.950
practically with yourself,

01:54:25.950 --> 01:54:28.980
trying to approach someone
with the chin slightly down,

01:54:28.980 --> 01:54:32.100
very linear, very efficient
in the straightest line,

01:54:32.100 --> 01:54:34.890
or trying to approach someone
a little bit more rounded

01:54:34.890 --> 01:54:37.710
from the side and tilting your head,

01:54:37.710 --> 01:54:40.560
and you will see totally
different results,

01:54:40.560 --> 01:54:42.910
totally different
communication that happens

01:54:44.010 --> 01:54:45.090
over people's heads.

01:54:45.090 --> 01:54:47.910
But if you're sensitive,
you realize that, "Wow,

01:54:47.910 --> 01:54:49.620
this opened the door."

01:54:49.620 --> 01:54:52.140
Instead, many people
you start on the minus.

01:54:52.140 --> 01:54:53.820
My sister, my big sister, Atali,

01:54:53.820 --> 01:54:56.700
she always says, "I started on the minus,

01:54:56.700 --> 01:54:59.130
why don't I start on zero with them?"

01:54:59.130 --> 01:55:02.610
You know, so, but it's
part of the approach.

01:55:02.610 --> 01:55:05.430
You can affect that, and you
can start even on the plus

01:55:05.430 --> 01:55:10.430
if you are the sly man as
the practitioner needs to be.

01:55:10.620 --> 01:55:14.310
So, this is something to
play with and to work with

01:55:14.310 --> 01:55:16.560
and then you have, of
course, body proportions,

01:55:16.560 --> 01:55:20.520
and ways, and we have all
these like technical invasions,

01:55:20.520 --> 01:55:23.580
mathematics, and trigonometry,
and architecture,

01:55:23.580 --> 01:55:27.270
they invaded our bodies, they
invaded our nervous system.

01:55:27.270 --> 01:55:30.090
And now, our walk and
our physical practices,

01:55:30.090 --> 01:55:32.700
they look linear and efficient,

01:55:32.700 --> 01:55:34.800
the path between two
points is a straight line,

01:55:34.800 --> 01:55:36.600
it's not, this is biomechanics,

01:55:36.600 --> 01:55:41.430
it's not mechanics,
nothing there is a given,

01:55:41.430 --> 01:55:43.260
there's no gospel.

01:55:43.260 --> 01:55:47.400
So, the walkers is
sometimes have to go around

01:55:47.400 --> 01:55:49.050
or sway from side to side,

01:55:49.050 --> 01:55:51.690
and there is coiling and coiling
and there are moving bits.

01:55:51.690 --> 01:55:53.760
And what about the coordination

01:55:53.760 --> 01:55:55.200
of my breathing with my walk?

01:55:55.200 --> 01:55:57.390
Because if I walk too linearly,

01:55:57.390 --> 01:56:00.360
there is less pumping of
the air naturally in and out

01:56:00.360 --> 01:56:02.100
so now I have to forcefully

01:56:02.100 --> 01:56:04.920
bring it in and out, I'm wasteful.

01:56:04.920 --> 01:56:07.770
And that's why you see in last years,

01:56:07.770 --> 01:56:12.030
these incredible runners,
especially in long distance,

01:56:12.030 --> 01:56:16.399
doing things we never
thought were possible

01:56:16.399 --> 01:56:21.399
in the worst possible way
that we used to think,

01:56:21.630 --> 01:56:23.883
pronation and all kinds of things

01:56:23.883 --> 01:56:26.220
like our technical thoughts

01:56:26.220 --> 01:56:30.990
were totally misguided and wrong.

01:56:30.990 --> 01:56:35.990
And then, somebody comes
in and does it in some way

01:56:36.150 --> 01:56:37.080
that is totally wrong

01:56:37.080 --> 01:56:40.620
and he gets results we could never get.

01:56:40.620 --> 01:56:43.350
And that's the beauty of playfulness,

01:56:43.350 --> 01:56:46.143
experimentation, change, being different.

01:56:48.180 --> 01:56:49.500
- As you're describing this I'm smiling

01:56:49.500 --> 01:56:52.320
because one of my
favorite neuroscientists,

01:56:52.320 --> 01:56:54.990
he's out of the university of Chicago,

01:56:54.990 --> 01:56:56.790
was in a meeting and there was an argument

01:56:56.790 --> 01:56:58.260
about evolution of the nervous system

01:56:58.260 --> 01:57:00.870
and he said, "At the end,"

01:57:00.870 --> 01:57:03.120
and people were arguing about
whether or not this gene

01:57:03.120 --> 01:57:04.710
in one animal was homologous

01:57:04.710 --> 01:57:06.300
to this gene in humans, et cetera,

01:57:06.300 --> 01:57:07.950
it can get very dicey.

01:57:07.950 --> 01:57:11.617
And he said very, very appropriately that,

01:57:11.617 --> 01:57:13.530
"One of the major jobs of evolution

01:57:13.530 --> 01:57:16.170
is to take existing
cell types and circuits

01:57:16.170 --> 01:57:17.760
and give them new functions.

01:57:17.760 --> 01:57:21.480
But that can only be done
through the playful exploration

01:57:21.480 --> 01:57:23.160
of new possibilities,"

01:57:23.160 --> 01:57:25.770
which I think maps very
well to what you're saying,

01:57:25.770 --> 01:57:29.910
that at the extreme thresholds
of technical execution,

01:57:29.910 --> 01:57:32.280
you know, mastery, mastery, mastery, you,

01:57:32.280 --> 01:57:34.950
your obviously performance is very high,

01:57:34.950 --> 01:57:37.710
but the opportunity for
evolution of the sport,

01:57:37.710 --> 01:57:40.650
or the music, or the dance,
or the intellectual endeavor

01:57:40.650 --> 01:57:44.070
is limited because you're
not introducing variability.

01:57:44.070 --> 01:57:46.800
In the attempt to get proper execution,

01:57:46.800 --> 01:57:49.020
you're limiting oneself.

01:57:49.020 --> 01:57:53.133
- Hence, I want to offer
something that is relating to you.

01:57:55.170 --> 01:57:56.340
We should be wary

01:57:56.340 --> 01:58:01.340
of defining the mechanisms

01:58:03.540 --> 01:58:08.540
and putting certain meaning
with certain processes and ways

01:58:08.970 --> 01:58:11.550
because just history and experience shows

01:58:11.550 --> 01:58:15.167
it doesn't work well for us most times,

01:58:15.167 --> 01:58:18.600
or it becomes like this
much more elaborate thing

01:58:18.600 --> 01:58:22.530
even if we were somewhat
in the right direction

01:58:22.530 --> 01:58:26.130
because even thinking
this way can offer a lot.

01:58:26.130 --> 01:58:28.260
Like for example, your advice

01:58:28.260 --> 01:58:30.510
about heat, dopamine, light,

01:58:30.510 --> 01:58:33.550
offers a lot of benefit,
but also can create problems

01:58:34.470 --> 01:58:38.400
and it can enclose something

01:58:38.400 --> 01:58:42.750
which the improviser

01:58:42.750 --> 01:58:44.700
will find, the MacGyvers, right?

01:58:44.700 --> 01:58:48.150
Like take a pink, some paper clip,

01:58:48.150 --> 01:58:51.150
and you make it into something great.

01:58:51.150 --> 01:58:53.163
And this is really our,

01:58:54.420 --> 01:58:56.370
we are the biggest improvisers around

01:58:56.370 --> 01:58:58.707
like that's what made
us who we are, I think,

01:58:58.707 --> 01:59:01.353
and this is incredible
what we can do with it.

01:59:02.460 --> 01:59:07.460
You know, the Russian-American
space exploration story

01:59:07.470 --> 01:59:08.910
with the space pen,

01:59:08.910 --> 01:59:12.420
famous story about the
development of the space pen.

01:59:12.420 --> 01:59:13.320
- I don't know.
- It's a-

01:59:13.320 --> 01:59:14.411
- A space pen?
- Yeah.

01:59:14.411 --> 01:59:15.410
- No, I don't know about this.

01:59:15.410 --> 01:59:17.780
- I think it's an urban myth.

01:59:17.780 --> 01:59:21.150
I don't know if it's true,
but I like it so I use it.

01:59:21.150 --> 01:59:25.710
Well, there was this, of
course, a space competition

01:59:25.710 --> 01:59:29.566
and the Russians put the
first animal in space

01:59:29.566 --> 01:59:31.070
and the first.
- He was a macaque monkey

01:59:31.070 --> 01:59:32.936
or something like that, yeah.
- Yeah.

01:59:32.936 --> 01:59:37.680
And then Laika, and they
put the first Sputnik,

01:59:37.680 --> 01:59:40.230
the satellite, and men in space,

01:59:40.230 --> 01:59:43.053
but Americans took the man on the moon.

01:59:43.890 --> 01:59:46.347
And on the way a lot of
technologies got developed

01:59:46.347 --> 01:59:49.170
and the Americans because
of lack of gravity out there

01:59:49.170 --> 01:59:51.930
developed the space pen
with a huge investment,

01:59:51.930 --> 01:59:53.801
the Russians used a pencil.

01:59:53.801 --> 01:59:56.550
[Andrew laughing]

01:59:56.550 --> 01:59:58.770
So, I don't know if it's true,

01:59:58.770 --> 01:59:59.610
I don't think it is,

01:59:59.610 --> 02:00:02.490
but it represents something
in the state of mind.

02:00:02.490 --> 02:00:03.630
Like you look at, for example,

02:00:03.630 --> 02:00:06.720
the military equipment
in Soviet equipment,

02:00:06.720 --> 02:00:09.570
it all can do multiple things

02:00:09.570 --> 02:00:11.100
and it means that it's heavier,

02:00:11.100 --> 02:00:14.130
it's less efficient, it's not as light,

02:00:14.130 --> 02:00:19.130
but even the Navy SEALs
will still carry an AK

02:00:19.320 --> 02:00:21.690
with certain conditions, why?

02:00:21.690 --> 02:00:23.760
Because you can pour
a whole bucket of sand

02:00:23.760 --> 02:00:26.130
into the mechanism and
it will keep running

02:00:26.130 --> 02:00:29.130
while the most advanced
German Heckler &amp; Koch

02:00:29.130 --> 02:00:31.740
and accurate and light weapons,

02:00:31.740 --> 02:00:35.190
for every grain can get
stuck and overly specialized.

02:00:35.190 --> 02:00:36.870
And there is something about this openness

02:00:36.870 --> 02:00:39.420
that we humans need to keep,

02:00:39.420 --> 02:00:42.390
and also maybe something for our leaders

02:00:42.390 --> 02:00:45.990
to be more of less specialists
and more in this openness,

02:00:45.990 --> 02:00:48.780
less capable in this or that way,

02:00:48.780 --> 02:00:51.123
but more capable of doing the whole thing.

02:00:53.670 --> 02:00:57.300
- I love the story, whether
or not it's a legend or not,

02:00:57.300 --> 02:01:00.480
it's legendary because it's fantastic.

02:01:00.480 --> 02:01:01.560
As you say, in the laboratory,

02:01:01.560 --> 02:01:03.420
whenever someone takes
on a project in my lab,

02:01:03.420 --> 02:01:05.610
I always say, "You know,
you have to ask yourself

02:01:05.610 --> 02:01:09.090
how much technical detail and
challenge you want to take on

02:01:09.090 --> 02:01:12.180
because with more technology,
advanced technology,

02:01:12.180 --> 02:01:13.980
yes, there's the opportunity
for more discovery,

02:01:13.980 --> 02:01:15.330
but more downtime.

02:01:15.330 --> 02:01:17.580
Your PhD will literally take longer

02:01:17.580 --> 02:01:18.870
if you're going to use a microscope

02:01:18.870 --> 02:01:21.720
that's out of commission 30% of the time,

02:01:21.720 --> 02:01:23.490
and you just have to understand that."

02:01:23.490 --> 02:01:26.310
So, there's a dynamic interplay there.

02:01:26.310 --> 02:01:29.010
- By the way, I think that
scientists get it right,

02:01:29.010 --> 02:01:30.917
it's where you transmit the knowledge

02:01:30.917 --> 02:01:32.910
out of the scientific field

02:01:32.910 --> 02:01:35.460
because science have
debate and everything,

02:01:35.460 --> 02:01:36.990
you're not so connected,

02:01:36.990 --> 02:01:38.310
of course this can happen as well,

02:01:38.310 --> 02:01:40.740
but then when it goes out

02:01:40.740 --> 02:01:45.510
and the simple person
without the experience

02:01:45.510 --> 02:01:48.120
takes it more as a
gospel, as a fixed thing,

02:01:48.120 --> 02:01:49.950
and then it was just a report.

02:01:49.950 --> 02:01:51.000
- Right.
- It was just reporting

02:01:51.000 --> 02:01:53.250
some functions here and play with it,

02:01:53.250 --> 02:01:55.133
see what it does for you.

02:01:55.133 --> 02:01:59.010
Because with all the greatest
information that I can give,

02:01:59.010 --> 02:02:00.267
the person will examine it

02:02:00.267 --> 02:02:04.020
and it might be not useful at all for him.

02:02:04.020 --> 02:02:06.750
This is the practitioner,
make it your own,

02:02:06.750 --> 02:02:10.740
go practice, try heat, cold, light,

02:02:10.740 --> 02:02:14.196
movement, awareness to
this, awareness to this.

02:02:14.196 --> 02:02:16.440
And this is up to you to make it yours,

02:02:16.440 --> 02:02:19.830
but we don't like to
have this responsibility.

02:02:19.830 --> 02:02:21.420
- No, people prefer to have

02:02:21.420 --> 02:02:24.150
that this will work the
first time every time

02:02:24.150 --> 02:02:27.690
and will serve you best
compared to everything else.

02:02:27.690 --> 02:02:31.500
And while there are more
reliable tools than others,

02:02:31.500 --> 02:02:33.600
in my mind, the more reliable tools

02:02:33.600 --> 02:02:38.110
tend to be ones that are
grounded in our innate physiology

02:02:39.510 --> 02:02:41.820
as opposed to some, I
don't like the word hack,

02:02:41.820 --> 02:02:43.710
in fact, I loathe the word biohack,

02:02:43.710 --> 02:02:45.930
as we were talking about again earlier,

02:02:45.930 --> 02:02:48.360
because a hack in my mind is something

02:02:48.360 --> 02:02:50.010
that is designed for one purpose

02:02:50.010 --> 02:02:51.587
that's used for another,

02:02:51.587 --> 02:02:54.600
it's not the most
efficient use of that tool,

02:02:54.600 --> 02:02:57.450
nor is it naturally the best solution.

02:02:57.450 --> 02:02:59.910
Whereas, biology has
some very good solutions,

02:02:59.910 --> 02:03:02.060
but they don't always
work, not every time.

02:03:03.600 --> 02:03:05.040
Earlier today, we did a practice

02:03:05.040 --> 02:03:08.790
in which involved a invasion,

02:03:08.790 --> 02:03:11.340
shall we say, of peripersonal space.

02:03:11.340 --> 02:03:14.190
We weren't standing super close
for any particular reason,

02:03:14.190 --> 02:03:16.740
but there was God forbid-
- God forbid.

02:03:16.740 --> 02:03:20.130
- But there was we were
close enough together

02:03:20.130 --> 02:03:21.780
we could touch one torsos

02:03:21.780 --> 02:03:24.150
and we were doing that
as part of this practice.

02:03:24.150 --> 02:03:26.130
And you encouraged me to pay attention

02:03:26.130 --> 02:03:27.780
to how does it feel

02:03:27.780 --> 02:03:30.300
to have someone in your
peripersonal space?

02:03:30.300 --> 02:03:32.070
And then, this notion of reactivity.

02:03:32.070 --> 02:03:34.170
I find this an immensely interesting

02:03:34.170 --> 02:03:36.720
and potentially powerful practice

02:03:36.720 --> 02:03:38.520
because I think a lot of people,

02:03:38.520 --> 02:03:41.790
I know a lot of people suffer from anxiety

02:03:41.790 --> 02:03:44.100
just being in a face-to-face conversation.

02:03:44.100 --> 02:03:46.020
Some people have a lot of anxiety

02:03:46.020 --> 02:03:47.700
about being physically close to people,

02:03:47.700 --> 02:03:49.560
whether or not they know them or not.

02:03:49.560 --> 02:03:51.330
And many people are reactive,

02:03:51.330 --> 02:03:53.160
they are in that anticipatory state

02:03:53.160 --> 02:03:54.570
of something that is going to happen.

02:03:54.570 --> 02:03:56.250
And sometimes this relates to trauma

02:03:56.250 --> 02:03:57.960
and negative experience, but sometimes no,

02:03:57.960 --> 02:04:01.230
sometimes they're just not
used to being in dynamic,

02:04:01.230 --> 02:04:04.680
excuse me, exchange with other beings.

02:04:04.680 --> 02:04:08.160
And so, one thing that I love
about the movement practice

02:04:08.160 --> 02:04:10.350
and how dynamic is that
one can explore that space.

02:04:10.350 --> 02:04:13.560
Maybe you could talk about
that a little bit more.

02:04:13.560 --> 02:04:14.393
- Yeah.

02:04:16.710 --> 02:04:21.270
Touch, proximity, all these things,

02:04:21.270 --> 02:04:23.193
also taking very,

02:04:25.260 --> 02:04:28.980
it takes a very, I think,
limited place in our lives.

02:04:28.980 --> 02:04:32.130
People are not touched and
they don't touch enough.

02:04:32.130 --> 02:04:35.550
There is certain bubbles
of peripersonal space

02:04:35.550 --> 02:04:37.920
according to culture,
according to environment,

02:04:37.920 --> 02:04:39.210
what is right, what is wrong?

02:04:39.210 --> 02:04:42.540
And then came all the, of
course, political correctness,

02:04:42.540 --> 02:04:45.330
and harassments, and all kinds.

02:04:45.330 --> 02:04:46.710
And this is a problem,

02:04:46.710 --> 02:04:50.190
it's a problem to navigate
all this scenario.

02:04:50.190 --> 02:04:51.690
And I think we are,

02:04:51.690 --> 02:04:55.410
there is definitely this
side which is suffering.

02:04:55.410 --> 02:04:58.257
People go to BJJ classes to touch,

02:04:58.257 --> 02:04:59.763
not to learn BJJ.

02:05:00.960 --> 02:05:03.150
Most of it, they're not even aware of it,

02:05:03.150 --> 02:05:06.033
before they would go
to a prostitute, maybe.

02:05:08.430 --> 02:05:10.833
It would not be honest to say that,

02:05:13.410 --> 02:05:16.983
yeah, this is not required or
necessary more in our lives.

02:05:18.270 --> 02:05:19.800
Children who are not touched,

02:05:19.800 --> 02:05:22.680
there is a lot of information
about that and the problems,

02:05:22.680 --> 02:05:24.540
but adults who are not touched,

02:05:24.540 --> 02:05:25.890
there is not a lot of information.

02:05:25.890 --> 02:05:28.020
And I think it's no less of a problem

02:05:28.020 --> 02:05:32.190
because it's something that
has to be constantly present.

02:05:32.190 --> 02:05:37.190
And then, proximity being
able to, as you said,

02:05:37.320 --> 02:05:41.110
remove certain reactivity
and to learn to control

02:05:43.410 --> 02:05:47.820
that volume control
over how reactive I am,

02:05:47.820 --> 02:05:49.140
and in other scenarios,

02:05:49.140 --> 02:05:51.300
how do I remove this reactivity altogether

02:05:51.300 --> 02:05:53.070
is very important for performance

02:05:53.070 --> 02:05:56.370
and also for our lives for
clear thinking, et cetera,

02:05:56.370 --> 02:05:58.590
because everything is moving through us

02:05:58.590 --> 02:06:00.190
and is being monitored by us

02:06:01.140 --> 02:06:03.900
so everything has the
potential to detract us

02:06:03.900 --> 02:06:07.200
from a certain direction of exploration

02:06:07.200 --> 02:06:11.048
or and if you're reactive, you're a slave,

02:06:11.048 --> 02:06:13.563
and it becomes worse and worse and worse.

02:06:14.520 --> 02:06:16.560
Or as, for example, a fighter,

02:06:16.560 --> 02:06:18.600
or a football player, et cetera,

02:06:18.600 --> 02:06:21.090
has to know what to
take, what not to take.

02:06:21.090 --> 02:06:22.980
The fact that you can sense more

02:06:22.980 --> 02:06:24.480
doesn't mean you should react to it.

02:06:24.480 --> 02:06:26.880
And the practice helps that

02:06:26.880 --> 02:06:29.190
by bringing people into these scenarios,

02:06:29.190 --> 02:06:31.350
but oftentimes disarming them.

02:06:31.350 --> 02:06:34.080
Like when we were working closely today,

02:06:34.080 --> 02:06:37.200
and because you have a certain background

02:06:37.200 --> 02:06:39.330
with boxing or fighting,

02:06:39.330 --> 02:06:43.650
I can tell you, you are
missing some kind of a way

02:06:43.650 --> 02:06:46.383
to be in that space that is not marshaled.

02:06:47.610 --> 02:06:50.340
So, you carry a certain tone,

02:06:50.340 --> 02:06:52.320
although you're a very kind person,

02:06:52.320 --> 02:06:54.810
but oftentimes you held me

02:06:54.810 --> 02:06:57.780
without realizing you're holding
me with a lot of strength,

02:06:57.780 --> 02:07:02.610
for example, and it just,

02:07:02.610 --> 02:07:04.890
it was clear to me you're not fully aware

02:07:04.890 --> 02:07:05.723
of what is unfolding

02:07:05.723 --> 02:07:09.420
and it's just, of course,
a question of experience.

02:07:09.420 --> 02:07:11.040
So, to be able to be in this scenario,

02:07:11.040 --> 02:07:12.930
but do something else,

02:07:12.930 --> 02:07:17.040
which is not geared towards
winning, losing competition,

02:07:17.040 --> 02:07:19.890
or just being able to
play with another person.

02:07:19.890 --> 02:07:22.950
Like for example, contact
improvisation took that

02:07:22.950 --> 02:07:24.237
and played with that,

02:07:24.237 --> 02:07:26.040
and the work of Steve Paxton

02:07:26.040 --> 02:07:28.623
for the ones who are not familiar.

02:07:29.670 --> 02:07:34.050
So, this is where I call it the hybrids

02:07:34.050 --> 02:07:35.640
become very useful,

02:07:35.640 --> 02:07:40.053
like we don't when you are
practicing in this open way,

02:07:41.040 --> 02:07:43.740
you are not bound by specific rule set

02:07:43.740 --> 02:07:45.000
or ways of doing things.

02:07:45.000 --> 02:07:50.000
It can be a fight, but it can be a dance

02:07:50.100 --> 02:07:51.030
a moment after.

02:07:51.030 --> 02:07:53.130
Another thing that I learned from capoeira

02:07:54.180 --> 02:07:55.920
the situation's very tricky there

02:07:55.920 --> 02:07:59.370
'cause I've seen kids
doing cartwheels in Brazil

02:07:59.370 --> 02:08:02.970
and scissors fall from their pocket.

02:08:02.970 --> 02:08:06.960
Why, why would you go to with
a scissor in your pocket?

02:08:06.960 --> 02:08:09.480
Obviously, there is certain intentions

02:08:09.480 --> 02:08:11.250
and then at other times you see back flips

02:08:11.250 --> 02:08:15.813
and beautiful things, but people
die in capoeira every year.

02:08:16.950 --> 02:08:18.690
- Neck breaks or something?

02:08:18.690 --> 02:08:23.100
- Kicks through the face
from various violence.

02:08:23.100 --> 02:08:27.360
It's I've explored other
martial arts and boxing,

02:08:27.360 --> 02:08:30.420
I was involved with MMA and BJJ,

02:08:30.420 --> 02:08:32.490
but I tell you the most violent arenas

02:08:32.490 --> 02:08:34.920
is that vibe 'cause it's unknown.

02:08:34.920 --> 02:08:36.570
One moment, it smiles,

02:08:36.570 --> 02:08:38.880
another moment, it's something
else and it's uncontrolled.

02:08:38.880 --> 02:08:40.530
There is no categories, no weights,

02:08:40.530 --> 02:08:42.690
and it's a street phenomenon.

02:08:42.690 --> 02:08:44.580
So, you have musical instruments,

02:08:44.580 --> 02:08:46.350
sometimes they break it on your head.

02:08:46.350 --> 02:08:47.340
People don't see that,

02:08:47.340 --> 02:08:49.470
but you can look online on YouTube

02:08:49.470 --> 02:08:51.810
and see some of that side of capoeira,

02:08:51.810 --> 02:08:56.340
which is actually the day-to-day in Brazil

02:08:56.340 --> 02:08:59.550
and the reality and how things unfolded.

02:08:59.550 --> 02:09:03.210
So, it's very important to explore

02:09:03.210 --> 02:09:07.230
many ways of being within
different distances and spaces

02:09:07.230 --> 02:09:10.380
from other people and
touched in different ways

02:09:10.380 --> 02:09:14.760
and not contextualizing
it always in the same way.

02:09:14.760 --> 02:09:17.640
I can touch your chest in one way,

02:09:17.640 --> 02:09:20.670
I can touch it with the exact
same pressure and speed,

02:09:20.670 --> 02:09:22.950
but it will feel very different.

02:09:22.950 --> 02:09:26.220
The parameters, I'm not
sure, certain intentions,

02:09:26.220 --> 02:09:29.460
certain combination of postures or ways,

02:09:29.460 --> 02:09:31.191
and this is beautiful exploration.

02:09:31.191 --> 02:09:36.120
And again, I would
encourage you and others

02:09:36.120 --> 02:09:38.160
to explore the discomfort.

02:09:38.160 --> 02:09:42.213
For example, certain
discomfort to be with a man

02:09:42.213 --> 02:09:44.310
in a certain scenario or with a woman

02:09:44.310 --> 02:09:46.980
and trying to see what is that?

02:09:46.980 --> 02:09:49.023
Because if we're truly strong,

02:09:49.890 --> 02:09:51.690
we are not afraid of anything.

02:09:51.690 --> 02:09:54.750
If we truly know who we are

02:09:54.750 --> 02:09:56.970
and we are in that exploration,

02:09:56.970 --> 02:09:58.470
we don't know the end result,

02:09:58.470 --> 02:10:00.150
but we are in a research

02:10:00.150 --> 02:10:03.180
and then we are not afraid
of being in debt or this,

02:10:03.180 --> 02:10:05.280
and we don't come out of boundaries,

02:10:05.280 --> 02:10:07.770
and this will improve
our culture tremendously.

02:10:07.770 --> 02:10:09.870
Of course, there must be agreement,

02:10:09.870 --> 02:10:10.950
you never force yourself,

02:10:10.950 --> 02:10:13.230
but you meet someone
who is also interested

02:10:13.230 --> 02:10:16.031
in that exploration and then you do it.

02:10:16.031 --> 02:10:18.450
And there are many scenarios

02:10:18.450 --> 02:10:20.820
to do that with traditional practices,

02:10:20.820 --> 02:10:22.620
like learning to grapple

02:10:22.620 --> 02:10:26.250
or going to contact improvisation

02:10:26.250 --> 02:10:28.860
and studying there, or going to dance,

02:10:28.860 --> 02:10:30.707
to Latin dance class

02:10:30.707 --> 02:10:33.270
or and there is, of course, my favorite

02:10:33.270 --> 02:10:37.620
is to create and to come
up with your own hybrids

02:10:37.620 --> 02:10:39.720
of that and scenarios.

02:10:39.720 --> 02:10:42.510
Communicating with your
loved one through movement,

02:10:42.510 --> 02:10:45.213
not sitting around food and talking,

02:10:46.170 --> 02:10:48.090
moving together in all kinds of ways.

02:10:48.090 --> 02:10:50.040
Sometimes it's walking together,

02:10:50.040 --> 02:10:53.700
but sometimes it's all
kinds of, it can be game,

02:10:53.700 --> 02:10:56.032
playful, it can be romantic.

02:10:56.032 --> 02:10:58.230
And there are many shades,

02:10:58.230 --> 02:11:01.680
sex doesn't start here
and end here, right?

02:11:01.680 --> 02:11:03.090
It's like a continuum,

02:11:03.090 --> 02:11:06.120
and we don't even need
to define it in that way.

02:11:06.120 --> 02:11:10.320
So, with time, I think it unlocks

02:11:10.320 --> 02:11:11.153
a lot of things.

02:11:11.153 --> 02:11:13.890
People become much
stronger in a good sense,

02:11:13.890 --> 02:11:16.920
in sense of becoming, being,

02:11:16.920 --> 02:11:20.733
and we abuse less, and we can approach,

02:11:22.380 --> 02:11:25.020
yeah, other aspects to us.

02:11:25.020 --> 02:11:27.300
- I love the idea that
through the exploration

02:11:27.300 --> 02:11:30.840
of a range of physical contacts

02:11:30.840 --> 02:11:33.330
provided one knows they can always return

02:11:33.330 --> 02:11:35.520
to their center, so to speak,

02:11:35.520 --> 02:11:38.700
then there's a lot of
opportunity that opens up.

02:11:38.700 --> 02:11:43.260
I wish there was more of that
encouraged in children's play,

02:11:43.260 --> 02:11:47.070
but also, as you mentioned,
in adult environments.

02:11:47.070 --> 02:11:51.060
Because yeah, nowadays
for all sorts of reasons

02:11:51.060 --> 02:11:52.950
that you've touched on,

02:11:52.950 --> 02:11:55.260
the idea of keeping at least
in arms length distance

02:11:55.260 --> 02:11:56.340
has become critical.

02:11:56.340 --> 02:11:57.210
There are a lot of environments

02:11:57.210 --> 02:11:59.910
actually where hugging is not allowed.

02:11:59.910 --> 02:12:01.500
I don't know what it's like in Israel,

02:12:01.500 --> 02:12:04.410
but in the states many
institutions you're not allowed

02:12:04.410 --> 02:12:06.930
to touch anyone else's body.

02:12:06.930 --> 02:12:08.040
There's actually a wonderful study

02:12:08.040 --> 02:12:10.020
that comes to mind from
an Israeli laboratory,

02:12:10.020 --> 02:12:12.543
a guy named Noam Sobel, who's over there,

02:12:13.920 --> 02:12:18.510
who has shown that by recording
people's first interactions

02:12:18.510 --> 02:12:22.500
that when people meet,
if they shake hands,

02:12:22.500 --> 02:12:24.300
they almost always,

02:12:24.300 --> 02:12:26.520
I think it's greater than 85% of the time

02:12:26.520 --> 02:12:28.050
they will then wipe the chemicals

02:12:28.050 --> 02:12:30.240
from the other person onto their own eyes,

02:12:30.240 --> 02:12:32.400
typically their eyes or their face.

02:12:32.400 --> 02:12:35.820
This changed a little bit
during the whole pandemic thing,

02:12:35.820 --> 02:12:39.780
but this is thought to be a carryover

02:12:39.780 --> 02:12:41.010
from what other animals do

02:12:41.010 --> 02:12:43.560
in terms of exchanging
microbiome elements,

02:12:43.560 --> 02:12:46.290
exchanging chemicals that
we're constantly feeding

02:12:46.290 --> 02:12:50.370
our subconscious with
the chemical knowledge

02:12:50.370 --> 02:12:54.240
of the chemical constituents
of other people, right?

02:12:54.240 --> 02:12:57.270
So, it goes way beyond how people smell,

02:12:57.270 --> 02:12:58.830
how they look, et cetera.

02:12:58.830 --> 02:13:01.830
More touch seems to me just, as you said,

02:13:01.830 --> 02:13:02.760
provided it's consensual,

02:13:02.760 --> 02:13:05.790
it seems like it's just a
really good thing overall.

02:13:05.790 --> 02:13:09.870
- And I think maybe also
important for discharging,

02:13:09.870 --> 02:13:13.500
discharging certain experiences,
remodeling, reframing.

02:13:13.500 --> 02:13:16.622
So, it's like touches,
it's very powerful in that.

02:13:16.622 --> 02:13:19.590
If you're touched and
you're touching a lot,

02:13:19.590 --> 02:13:22.560
you're unpacking and you
experience that touch

02:13:22.560 --> 02:13:26.100
that maybe has been traumatic
and you're reframing it,

02:13:26.100 --> 02:13:31.050
you have the opportunity,
which is something interesting.

02:13:31.050 --> 02:13:34.500
I've heard some story

02:13:34.500 --> 02:13:36.660
about some traditional culture

02:13:36.660 --> 02:13:39.690
in which when you were burned by mistake,

02:13:39.690 --> 02:13:41.590
they would immediately burn you again.

02:13:43.079 --> 02:13:44.127
And it made me think,

02:13:44.127 --> 02:13:46.630
and then there would not be any burn marks

02:13:47.790 --> 02:13:50.940
and there would not be
the same side effects,

02:13:50.940 --> 02:13:51.993
that's the claim.

02:13:53.010 --> 02:13:55.380
It made me think it's like,
"What's the source of this?"

02:13:55.380 --> 02:13:58.260
And I realized that maybe it allows

02:13:58.260 --> 02:14:01.050
a certain completion to happen

02:14:01.050 --> 02:14:04.080
that in the traumatic moment is not there.

02:14:04.080 --> 02:14:07.020
So, the re-exposure,
while you're still open,

02:14:07.020 --> 02:14:08.880
the pores are still open,

02:14:08.880 --> 02:14:11.400
allows you to reframe the experience

02:14:11.400 --> 02:14:15.060
and then the unfolding
of the rest of the event

02:14:15.060 --> 02:14:16.590
is very different.

02:14:16.590 --> 02:14:18.210
This is if you're touching

02:14:18.210 --> 02:14:19.800
in your practice in the day-to-day,

02:14:19.800 --> 02:14:21.720
and you're working with people,
and you're being touched,

02:14:21.720 --> 02:14:25.083
and people come closer, further
away, it happens naturally.

02:14:27.390 --> 02:14:30.180
Yeah, and if you pass a certain limit

02:14:30.180 --> 02:14:32.370
and it becomes too much,

02:14:32.370 --> 02:14:34.440
there is always, of course, communication

02:14:34.440 --> 02:14:36.540
that has to be present.

02:14:36.540 --> 02:14:40.710
Certain cultures make
this communication pre,

02:14:40.710 --> 02:14:43.350
certain cultures post.

02:14:43.350 --> 02:14:45.750
The Israeli, for example, post,

02:14:45.750 --> 02:14:46.980
here pre.

02:14:46.980 --> 02:14:47.813
- Ah, so in Israel,

02:14:47.813 --> 02:14:49.800
they'll say, "That
didn't feel good to me,"

02:14:49.800 --> 02:14:51.930
or, "That felt good," or, "That was fine."

02:14:51.930 --> 02:14:53.703
- Yeah, it would be more common.

02:14:55.200 --> 02:14:56.730
Here in the airport,

02:14:56.730 --> 02:14:59.040
the guys telling me, "I'm
going to slide my hands

02:14:59.040 --> 02:15:02.997
up towards your crotch
until I meet the hard stop,"

02:15:03.951 --> 02:15:08.070
and then he does this in a way
that is supposed to show me,

02:15:08.070 --> 02:15:10.950
I have no enjoyment in that

02:15:10.950 --> 02:15:13.143
and for me it just feels aggressive.

02:15:14.070 --> 02:15:17.160
But his intention is good, showing me.

02:15:17.160 --> 02:15:19.110
But if it was a loving touch,

02:15:19.110 --> 02:15:21.480
it would be nicer for me actually,

02:15:21.480 --> 02:15:24.600
personally that's it would be gentle,

02:15:24.600 --> 02:15:26.947
but he goes up there and he shows me,

02:15:26.947 --> 02:15:28.587
"I have no enjoyment in this."

02:15:29.927 --> 02:15:33.450
[groans] That's my testicle right there,

02:15:33.450 --> 02:15:37.290
so it's different choices.
[Andrew laughing]

02:15:37.290 --> 02:15:38.697
I don't think it's like worse,

02:15:38.697 --> 02:15:42.390
but this description can
be a bit dissociated.

02:15:42.390 --> 02:15:44.010
And what does it make me think?

02:15:44.010 --> 02:15:46.277
Is it truly what he feels or not?

02:15:46.277 --> 02:15:49.500
'Cause it feels robotic, so it's not.

02:15:49.500 --> 02:15:51.600
So, sometimes I'd rather not say it

02:15:51.600 --> 02:15:54.360
and I'm going to touch your chest

02:15:54.360 --> 02:15:57.000
and just place my hand on the chest.

02:15:57.000 --> 02:16:01.020
And, of course I, we
can't avoid a problem,

02:16:01.020 --> 02:16:02.520
I'm not suggesting that there is,

02:16:02.520 --> 02:16:04.140
but there is an examination.

02:16:04.140 --> 02:16:06.840
And because I moved around
and around the world,

02:16:06.840 --> 02:16:07.770
I've seen many things

02:16:07.770 --> 02:16:10.593
and I've seen benefits
here and benefits there.

02:16:12.180 --> 02:16:15.180
And in the practice, I think
it's important to discuss this,

02:16:15.180 --> 02:16:16.980
to examine this, I don't have a solution,

02:16:16.980 --> 02:16:19.650
but it's something to talk about.

02:16:19.650 --> 02:16:20.970
- It is something to talk about,

02:16:20.970 --> 02:16:21.930
and I'm glad you raised it

02:16:21.930 --> 02:16:24.540
because I think that it's so clear to me

02:16:24.540 --> 02:16:27.420
that much of the value
of a movement practice

02:16:27.420 --> 02:16:29.850
involves this dynamic
interaction with somebody else.

02:16:29.850 --> 02:16:32.400
As you pointed out, it can
be performed on one's own

02:16:32.400 --> 02:16:34.470
and practiced throughout one's day.

02:16:34.470 --> 02:16:39.090
But the unpredictability is
a key element to all of it,

02:16:39.090 --> 02:16:42.180
and in bringing out all the potential

02:16:42.180 --> 02:16:43.473
that you've described.

02:16:44.520 --> 02:16:46.650
In reference to this notion of trauma

02:16:46.650 --> 02:16:48.540
and burn and re-burn.

02:16:48.540 --> 02:16:50.490
My colleague at Stanford, David Spiegel,

02:16:50.490 --> 02:16:52.560
he works on trauma

02:16:52.560 --> 02:16:55.230
and has actually on this podcast,

02:16:55.230 --> 02:16:58.110
he voiced that he's against
things like trigger warnings

02:16:58.110 --> 02:16:59.880
because of the way that
it puts the nervous system

02:16:59.880 --> 02:17:02.580
into this state of
readiness and reactivity

02:17:02.580 --> 02:17:05.310
that can exacerbate problems.

02:17:05.310 --> 02:17:09.570
Whereas, it's very clear
from the literature on trauma

02:17:09.570 --> 02:17:12.150
and trauma relief that
the way to deal with that

02:17:12.150 --> 02:17:14.070
is through a controlled,

02:17:14.070 --> 02:17:17.700
but clearly a controlled
re-exposure to the trauma

02:17:17.700 --> 02:17:20.610
in order to diminish the
emotional response over time.

02:17:20.610 --> 02:17:23.280
I mean, it's very clear
if we avoid the thing,

02:17:23.280 --> 02:17:25.140
obviously we don't want
to re-injure ourselves

02:17:25.140 --> 02:17:26.160
or re-traumatize,

02:17:26.160 --> 02:17:28.140
but if one avoids the
thing that makes them upset

02:17:28.140 --> 02:17:30.060
over and over, all it
does is serve to create

02:17:30.060 --> 02:17:33.180
a heightened state of readiness,
it primes more trauma.

02:17:33.180 --> 02:17:34.050
- Yeah.
- So, I think

02:17:34.050 --> 02:17:34.950
it makes good sense.

02:17:34.950 --> 02:17:37.980
- I think impressions are very useful here

02:17:37.980 --> 02:17:42.300
also when stepping into an
area in which trauma can occur.

02:17:42.300 --> 02:17:46.200
And then, by going through the impression

02:17:46.200 --> 02:17:47.760
that it already occurred,

02:17:47.760 --> 02:17:50.763
you create some kind of a
thermal layer of protection,

02:17:52.230 --> 02:17:56.640
so I've already been hit
when I'm entering that space,

02:17:56.640 --> 02:17:58.470
it's so beneficial.

02:17:58.470 --> 02:18:01.530
Or I've already been touched
in a way that I didn't like

02:18:01.530 --> 02:18:03.990
if I go to a contact improvisation class,

02:18:03.990 --> 02:18:06.550
and just running this
scenario in your head

02:18:07.680 --> 02:18:11.160
protects so well, yeah.

02:18:11.160 --> 02:18:13.140
- I'm glad you mentioned
running scenarios in your head.

02:18:13.140 --> 02:18:15.810
I've been curious all
day as to whether or not

02:18:15.810 --> 02:18:17.670
you do visualization

02:18:17.670 --> 02:18:20.163
or mental rehearsal of physical movement.

02:18:21.120 --> 02:18:24.600
This is it seems to be a
popular idea in the states,

02:18:24.600 --> 02:18:26.310
people are always asking me, "You know,

02:18:26.310 --> 02:18:28.500
can you just imagine a movement

02:18:28.500 --> 02:18:32.430
and learn it better than where
you to actually perform it?"

02:18:32.430 --> 02:18:34.620
My hunch based on and my understanding

02:18:34.620 --> 02:18:37.470
of the scientific literature
is that visualization

02:18:37.470 --> 02:18:39.330
can be useful to some extent

02:18:39.330 --> 02:18:41.790
for people that are very
good at visualization,

02:18:41.790 --> 02:18:43.920
but for many people, it doesn't help.

02:18:43.920 --> 02:18:47.430
And that there's nothing
like real physical practice

02:18:47.430 --> 02:18:50.670
to improve physical practice.

02:18:50.670 --> 02:18:54.630
- Yeah, the word visualization's
not good, obviously,

02:18:54.630 --> 02:18:58.060
it has to be experientialization

02:18:59.190 --> 02:19:02.283
in a very complete way, not
just visually, of course.

02:19:03.870 --> 02:19:07.990
And unless you already developed

02:19:09.090 --> 02:19:11.513
certain experience, tangible experience

02:19:11.513 --> 02:19:16.143
that has benefited from
feedback, from outside feedback.

02:19:17.160 --> 02:19:20.037
It is not a very useful thing to do,

02:19:20.037 --> 02:19:24.420
and it ends up being fabrications.

02:19:24.420 --> 02:19:25.890
But if you are very experienced

02:19:25.890 --> 02:19:29.190
and you already gained the
benefit of being burnt here

02:19:29.190 --> 02:19:31.020
or overextended here,

02:19:31.020 --> 02:19:32.550
then you have a certain experience

02:19:32.550 --> 02:19:35.430
and then you can strengthen
certain aspects of it,

02:19:35.430 --> 02:19:38.330
but you got to be careful
because you do not have feedback.

02:19:39.330 --> 02:19:41.070
And because of the missing feedback,

02:19:41.070 --> 02:19:43.140
you might develop delusions.

02:19:43.140 --> 02:19:46.590
It might be that you
develop stronger patterning,

02:19:46.590 --> 02:19:49.680
but ultimately this would lead you away

02:19:49.680 --> 02:19:54.390
from the aliveness of the movement itself.

02:19:54.390 --> 02:19:59.340
Drilling, for example,
very useful to learn

02:19:59.340 --> 02:20:03.150
a general infrastructure
of the movement sleeve

02:20:03.150 --> 02:20:06.120
or the technique, but then to dress it up,

02:20:06.120 --> 02:20:09.480
you need feedback, you
need it to be alive,

02:20:09.480 --> 02:20:14.190
you need to receive something corrective.

02:20:14.190 --> 02:20:15.810
- I love it.

02:20:15.810 --> 02:20:18.300
For many people, they approach movement

02:20:18.300 --> 02:20:21.483
in the form of weight
training, or yoga, or running.

02:20:22.560 --> 02:20:24.060
Yoga's a bit more dynamic,

02:20:24.060 --> 02:20:29.060
but it fairly linear types
of exercise and movement,

02:20:29.730 --> 02:20:33.090
Peloton, rowing, those kinds of things.

02:20:33.090 --> 02:20:37.050
I think most people
will probably not depart

02:20:37.050 --> 02:20:39.720
from those practices entirely
because they like them.

02:20:39.720 --> 02:20:41.190
I'm speaking about myself,

02:20:41.190 --> 02:20:43.440
I like some of those
very much, I enjoy them.

02:20:44.310 --> 02:20:47.730
But in terms of thinking about
adding a movement practice

02:20:47.730 --> 02:20:51.093
to one's already existing exercise regime,

02:20:52.800 --> 02:20:54.390
I can imagine threading
it throughout the day,

02:20:54.390 --> 02:20:56.790
I can imagine having a
dedicated movement practice.

02:20:56.790 --> 02:20:59.760
One thing that I have started doing

02:20:59.760 --> 02:21:01.860
on the basis of some of your teachings,

02:21:01.860 --> 02:21:03.240
and I just sort of created this idea

02:21:03.240 --> 02:21:04.740
is rather than statically

02:21:04.740 --> 02:21:05.910
standing there and lifting weights,

02:21:05.910 --> 02:21:08.670
actually walking from, as
I alternate repetitions,

02:21:08.670 --> 02:21:12.600
it occurred to me that
I'd never done a curl,

02:21:12.600 --> 02:21:15.960
a bicep curl with one foot
in front of the other,

02:21:15.960 --> 02:21:18.840
and then I'd never
actually switched that up.

02:21:18.840 --> 02:21:21.420
And it's kind of an odd stance
to be standing in parallel

02:21:21.420 --> 02:21:22.290
and curling one's arm,

02:21:22.290 --> 02:21:24.840
it's kind of a ridiculous
movement when one thinks about it.

02:21:24.840 --> 02:21:26.130
So, I started incorporating some of that,

02:21:26.130 --> 02:21:27.570
you get some strange looks in the gym,

02:21:27.570 --> 02:21:29.760
but I just give them strange looks back.

02:21:29.760 --> 02:21:32.340
So, what are your thoughts
[Ido laughing]

02:21:32.340 --> 02:21:34.522
about these very linear forms of exercise?

02:21:34.522 --> 02:21:37.800
And, do you encourage people

02:21:37.800 --> 02:21:41.190
to expand the play space, as it were,

02:21:41.190 --> 02:21:43.050
for these kinds of exercise?

02:21:43.050 --> 02:21:47.130
Or do you think that movement
practice is just best explored

02:21:47.130 --> 02:21:50.070
through three dimensionality,

02:21:50.070 --> 02:21:53.085
gravity, and maybe a stick or a ball?

02:21:53.085 --> 02:21:56.340
- Hmm, it's definitely a problem,

02:21:56.340 --> 02:21:58.170
and it's approachable.

02:21:58.170 --> 02:22:00.930
People want a quick, people want a hack,

02:22:00.930 --> 02:22:03.963
people want the icing, there is no cake.

02:22:04.950 --> 02:22:08.130
There is no cake, and it's
just like industries of icing,

02:22:08.130 --> 02:22:09.810
icing, icing on what?

02:22:09.810 --> 02:22:11.310
What are you putting it on?

02:22:11.310 --> 02:22:16.170
So, for me that, that's why
I'm going towards this side,

02:22:16.170 --> 02:22:18.120
it's like I have my life,

02:22:18.120 --> 02:22:21.183
now tell me what movement
practices I should pursue.

02:22:22.320 --> 02:22:23.553
You are movement,

02:22:24.600 --> 02:22:28.170
in essence, you are not
thinking of yourself

02:22:28.170 --> 02:22:32.073
in any serious way through my eyes.

02:22:33.000 --> 02:22:36.000
There is a dynamic entity to you,

02:22:36.000 --> 02:22:39.810
the body is a huge part
of it communicating,

02:22:39.810 --> 02:22:42.570
you have genetic layers,

02:22:42.570 --> 02:22:45.240
there is a personalities
that got developed

02:22:45.240 --> 02:22:47.460
and built around various influences,

02:22:47.460 --> 02:22:49.950
but then there is also
some kind of an essence,

02:22:49.950 --> 02:22:53.700
something that reeks
from within the cells.

02:22:53.700 --> 02:22:56.460
And if you grew up in my family

02:22:56.460 --> 02:22:59.700
and I grew up in your family
and it would still be the same,

02:22:59.700 --> 02:23:02.520
and that it's something that
I always try to think about,

02:23:02.520 --> 02:23:04.083
what is that inside of me?

02:23:06.480 --> 02:23:09.360
So, I think these practices,
they're very good,

02:23:09.360 --> 02:23:12.030
but they're not designed for the goal

02:23:12.030 --> 02:23:14.833
that we think they were designed to do it,

02:23:15.810 --> 02:23:17.400
it orients towards something else.

02:23:17.400 --> 02:23:19.380
For example, yoga,

02:23:19.380 --> 02:23:22.140
there is a good book
called the "Yoga Body",

02:23:22.140 --> 02:23:25.293
which will destroy a lot
of people's yoga practice.

02:23:26.334 --> 02:23:29.610
And it goes into how
did we get to this yoga,

02:23:29.610 --> 02:23:32.160
the influence of Swedish gymnastics

02:23:32.160 --> 02:23:35.940
and Mongolian contortionists,

02:23:35.940 --> 02:23:40.590
and the Western, the West affecting it,

02:23:40.590 --> 02:23:42.120
and then the ancient practice,

02:23:42.120 --> 02:23:46.230
which was barely asana-related,

02:23:46.230 --> 02:23:47.280
posture, position.

02:23:47.280 --> 02:23:49.500
So, actually you said yoga is less linear,

02:23:49.500 --> 02:23:53.550
yoga is very linear, very
linear these days, these lines.

02:23:53.550 --> 02:23:55.470
Look at all the traditional dances,

02:23:55.470 --> 02:23:58.050
they look like nothing like yoga.

02:23:58.050 --> 02:24:02.250
Look at tai dance, look at
Chinese dances, martial arts,

02:24:02.250 --> 02:24:03.660
it's all rounded, it's all curved,

02:24:03.660 --> 02:24:05.490
it's like the like out nature,

02:24:05.490 --> 02:24:09.330
what you see in nature and
the movement of the animals.

02:24:09.330 --> 02:24:11.820
So, where does it come from?

02:24:11.820 --> 02:24:13.230
These are things to understand

02:24:13.230 --> 02:24:16.050
because it designs you now, it shapes you.

02:24:16.050 --> 02:24:19.620
You're placing yourself
in these forces of change

02:24:19.620 --> 02:24:21.510
and these streams of change,

02:24:21.510 --> 02:24:22.830
and you have a good intention,

02:24:22.830 --> 02:24:27.090
you just want this or that,
but the joke is on us.

02:24:27.090 --> 02:24:32.010
And this is the movement practice
for me is first education,

02:24:32.010 --> 02:24:33.600
let's start to think about this.

02:24:33.600 --> 02:24:37.830
I have nothing that I
can just sprinkle now,

02:24:37.830 --> 02:24:41.040
some magic powder that
will help resolve this

02:24:41.040 --> 02:24:43.803
because it's a start of
a deep investigation.

02:24:45.300 --> 02:24:48.510
And then, some of the things,
let's talk pragmatically,

02:24:48.510 --> 02:24:50.430
because what you described

02:24:50.430 --> 02:24:52.830
is not about you placing the foot in front

02:24:52.830 --> 02:24:56.430
when you're curling, it's
about the examination,

02:24:56.430 --> 02:25:00.210
this is why it is a very good direction.

02:25:00.210 --> 02:25:02.460
And then, you will need
another one, another one,

02:25:02.460 --> 02:25:04.800
don't get stuck on that
foot in front of it

02:25:04.800 --> 02:25:06.930
and try to do with the eyes closed

02:25:06.930 --> 02:25:08.190
or with a different head posture,

02:25:08.190 --> 02:25:10.830
and you will see things arrive,

02:25:10.830 --> 02:25:13.980
unrelated things because
the associative mind,

02:25:13.980 --> 02:25:15.330
the thinking, this relates,

02:25:15.330 --> 02:25:17.480
this doesn't get to
the heart of it, never.

02:25:18.600 --> 02:25:21.640
So, just infusing

02:25:23.220 --> 02:25:26.070
these elements like in a cup

02:25:26.070 --> 02:25:29.430
will create endless combinations,

02:25:29.430 --> 02:25:31.950
possibilities, and a lot of discovery.

02:25:31.950 --> 02:25:34.530
And this for me is humility
of the practitioner.

02:25:34.530 --> 02:25:37.290
I don't know, I try like today with you.,

02:25:37.290 --> 02:25:39.720
I tried various combinations

02:25:39.720 --> 02:25:41.160
and oh, I discover something,

02:25:41.160 --> 02:25:43.350
oh, there is, this is a playful approach,

02:25:43.350 --> 02:25:45.513
and this is a researcher approach.

02:25:46.590 --> 02:25:49.377
I don't try to fit my
truth into something,

02:25:49.377 --> 02:25:51.870
I'm there to examine.

02:25:51.870 --> 02:25:54.153
I don't have a motive yet, why?

02:25:55.050 --> 02:25:59.370
Because I'm fine, I don't
depend on that to define myself.

02:25:59.370 --> 02:26:00.870
I'm a human being,

02:26:00.870 --> 02:26:02.602
but if I don't have that sense of worth,

02:26:02.602 --> 02:26:06.150
I'm already like geared
towards I need to do this,

02:26:06.150 --> 02:26:08.370
I need to prove this, I have this agenda.

02:26:08.370 --> 02:26:10.920
And this is how we get
all the lies in the world

02:26:10.920 --> 02:26:13.740
and all the problems and difficulties.

02:26:13.740 --> 02:26:17.580
So, these practices,
they are related to it

02:26:17.580 --> 02:26:19.863
to prove this, that, this way,

02:26:20.940 --> 02:26:23.970
why we need muscles for X, Y, Z.

02:26:23.970 --> 02:26:28.970
And a lot of the reported outcomes

02:26:29.490 --> 02:26:32.430
are often from my place is like funny.

02:26:32.430 --> 02:26:34.980
I hear about something
like I heard you say

02:26:34.980 --> 02:26:36.550
